= Markov process
{wiki}

A Markov process is a <stochastic process> for which, conditional on the present state, the future is independent of the past. Its transition probabilities therefore determine every finite-dimensional distribution once the initial distribution is known.

= Markov operator
{parent=Markov process}
{c}
{wiki}

A positive <linear operator> on bounded <measurable functions> that preserves the constant function one. A <Markov semigroup> is a time-indexed family of such operators with the semigroup law.

= Markov semigroup
{parent=Markov operator}
{c}
{title2=$P_tg(x)=\mathbb E_xg(X_t)$}

= Transition semigroup
{synonym}

The transition operators of a time-homogeneous <Markov process> satisfy $P_{t+s}=P_tP_s$ by the <Markov property>. They preserve constants and positivity. When the generator and function domain permit differentiation, $\partial_tP_tg=L(P_tg)$ is the <Kolmogorov backward equation>. <Transition semigroup of a continuous-time Markov chain> is the discrete-state special case.

= Invariant probability law of a Markov process
{parent=Markov process}
{title2=$\nu P_t=\nu\quad(t\ge0)$}

= Invariant probability measure of a Markov process
{synonym}

A <probability measure> is invariant for a <Markov process> with transition semigroup $P_t$ when starting with that law gives the same law at every later time. Equivalently, $\int P_tf\,d\nu=\int f\,d\nu$ for every bounded measurable function. Invariant laws may be constructed by <weak convergence of probability measures> of evolved laws when the <Feller semigroup> property permits passing the semigroup through the limit, as for the <extremal invariant measures of the contact process>.

= Affine process
{parent=Markov process}
{title2=$\log\mathbb E_x e^{\theta X_1}=A(\theta)x+B(\theta)$}

An affine process is a time-homogeneous <Markov process> whose conditional <moment-generating function> is exponential-affine in its starting state. In discrete time one writes $\log\mathbb E_x e^{\theta X_1}=A(\theta)x+B(\theta)$ on the finite <exponential moment> domain. Continuous-time definitions allow the functions to depend also on elapsed time.

= Affine autoregressive process
{parent=Affine process}
{title2=$X_t=aX_{t-1}+b+\xi_t$}

An <autoregressive process of order one> with <independent and identically distributed random variables> as innovations is an <affine process> when the innovation <exponential moments> are finite. Its one-step coefficients are $A(\theta)=a\theta$ and $B(\theta)=b\theta+\psi(\theta)$, where $\psi$ is the innovation <cumulant-generating function>.

= Joint affine transform
{parent=Affine process}

For a scalar discrete-time <affine process>, the conditional <cumulant-generating function> of $\sum_{j=1}^t\theta_jX_j$ is affine in $X_0$. Backward effective parameters $q_t=\theta_t$, $q_j=\theta_j+A(q_{j+1})$ give coefficients $A(q_1)$ and $\sum_jB(q_j)$.

= Doob h-transform
{c}
{parent=Markov process}
{title2=$P_t^hf(x)=h(x)^{-1}P_t(hf)(x)$}

For a killed <Markov process> and a positive harmonic function $h$, its Doob h-transform weights the path law through a stopping time $\tau$ by $h(X_\tau)/h(X_0)$, with localization when necessary. It describes conditioning on a boundary behaviour of probability zero. In the <complex upper half-plane>, $h(z)=\operatorname{Im}z$ conditions killed <planar Brownian motion> to remain in that half-plane.

= Brownian change of measure by a positive space-time harmonic function
{c}
{parent=Doob h-transform}
{title2=$dX_t=dB_t^{\mathbb Q}+\partial_x\log h(X_t,t)dt$}

If $h>0$ is smooth and $Z_t=h(X_t,t)$ is the unit-mean density martingale of a locally absolutely continuous probability measure relative to Wiener measure, then $h_t+\frac12h_{xx}=0$. The <Itô formula> gives $Z=\mathcal E(\int(h_x/h)(X_s,s)dX_s)$ when $Z_0=1$. The true-martingale hypothesis is already supplied by the density process. On each finite horizon the <Girsanov theorem> makes $X-\int(h_x/h)(X_s,s)ds$ Brownian under the new measure. Positivity provides local equivalence; global infinite-horizon equivalence and the Novikov condition are not additional requirements.

= Brownian motion conditioned to exit at a boundary point
{c}
{parent=Doob h-transform}
{title2=$h(z)=P_D(z,a)/P_D(z_0,a)$}

Killed <planar Brownian motion> conditioned to exit at a smooth boundary point $a$ is the <Doob h-transform> by its positive <Poisson kernel>, normalized at the starting point. Its generator is $\tfrac12\Delta+\nabla\log h\cdot\nabla$. Conditioning on shrinking boundary arcs yields the same localized laws and specifies a canonical version of an otherwise probability-zero conditioning event.

= Radial restriction for a conditioned Brownian path
{parent=Brownian motion conditioned to exit at a boundary point}
{title2=$\mathbb P(B\subset U)=|\Phi_U'(1)|$}

For a Brownian path in the unit disc from $0$ conditioned to exit at $1$, let $\Phi_U:U\to D$ fix $0$ and $1$, and suppose $U$ agrees with the disc near $1$. The probability of avoiding $D\setminus U$ is the ratio of the boundary Poisson-kernel densities, hence $|\Phi_U'(1)|$. Given avoidance, the conformally mapped path has the original law up to the <conformal Brownian clock>. There is no conformal-radius factor at the interior starting point.

= Hidden Markov model
{c}
{parent=Markov process}
{wiki}

A hidden Markov model is a <Markov process> whose state is latent and observed only through random emissions whose conditional distribution depends on the current hidden state.

= Screening misclassification model
{parent=Hidden Markov model}

In a <screening misclassification model>, true disease states are latent and screen results are emissions. A cancer-free state may emit a positive result with a <false-positive rate>, while a perfectly sensitive pre-clinical state always emits a positive result. Conditional independence of emissions given latent states is an additional model assumption, not a consequence of a marginal false-positive rate.

= Chapman-Kolmogorov equation
{c}
{parent=Markov process}
{wiki}

For a time-homogeneous Markov process with transition kernel $P_t(x,dy)$,
$$
P_{s+t}(x,A)=\int P_s(x,dy)P_t(y,A).
$$
When transition densities exist, this becomes
$$
p(s+t,x,z)=\int p(s,x,y)p(t,y,z)dy.
$$

= Markov chain
{parent=Markov process}
{c}
{wiki}

A Markov chain has a future conditional distribution depending on the present state alone.

= Two-lane Markov chain with a deterministic restart
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$q_c=(\sqrt5-1)/2$}

Take states $0,n,n'$ for $n\geq1$, with $0\to1'$ deterministic and transitions $n\to n-1$ with probability $q$, $n\to(n+1)'$ with probability $p=1-q$, $n'\to n$ with probability $q$, and $n'\to(n+1)'$ with probability $p$. The <Markov chain> is transient for $q<q_c$, null recurrent for $q=q_c$, and positive recurrent for $q>q_c$. To see the <Markov chain> recurrence threshold, eliminate the primed-state <hitting probabilities> to obtain $p(1+q)a_{n+1}-(1+pq)a_n+qa_{n-1}=0$, with characteristic roots $1$ and $q/(1-q^2)$. Boundedness forces return with probability one when the second root is at least one. When it is below one, the minimal nonnegative solution gives return probability $q^2/p<1$ from $0$. In the positive recurrent case, put $t=(1-q^2)/q$ and $D=q^2+q-1$. The <stationary distribution> is $\pi_0=D/[q(q+2)]$, $\pi_n=(\pi_0/q)t^{n-1}$ and $\pi_{n'}=\pi_0t^{n-1}$. It follows from balance across each level cut and sums to one; at criticality the invariant weights cannot be normalized.

= Jump chain of a discrete-time Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$\widetilde P_{ij}=P_{ij}/(1-P_{ii}),\quad i\ne j$}

= Discrete-time jump chain
{synonym}

For $P_{ii}<1$, observe a discrete-time <Markov chain> only when its state changes. The <probability> that its next different state is $j$ is $\sum_{r\geq0}P_{ii}^rP_{ij}=P_{ij}/(1-P_{ii})$. Its <holding times> are finite geometric waiting times. This construction deletes self-transitions and differs from the existing continuous-time jump-chain concept.

= Recurrence survives deletion of self-transitions
{parent=Jump chain of a discrete-time Markov chain}

An irreducible recurrent discrete-time <Markov chain> with at least two states has $P_{ii}<1$ everywhere. Removing repeated consecutive states from a positive-<probability> path proves irreducibility of its <discrete-time jump chain>. Each sojourn in a state is almost surely finite; infinitely many visits to a <recurrent state> therefore correspond to infinitely many distinct returning sojourns. The jump chain remains recurrent, although deleting <holding times> may change periodicity.

= Reflected chain with geometric downward jumps
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$X_{n+1}=\max(0,X_n+1-D_n)$}

Let independent $D_n$ have $\mathbb P(D_n=k)=pq^k$, $k\geq0$, $q=1-p$. The displayed chain has transition probabilities $P_{ij}=pq^{i-j+1}$ for $1\leq j\leq i+1$ and $P_{i0}=q^{i+1}$. Its <probability> of hitting zero from $i\geq1$ is one when $p\leq1/2$ and $(q/p)^{i+1}$ when $p>1/2$. The first-step boundary equation at $i=1$ is essential for the extra exponent. The chain is <positive recurrent> for $p<1/2$, with stationary probabilities $(1-p/q)(p/q)^j$, <null recurrent> at $p=1/2$, and transient for $p>1/2$.

= Last-excursion direction law at a transient junction
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$\Pr(\text{escape through branch }j)=a_j/\sum_i a_i$}

At a junction, let $a_j$ be the probability that one excursion leaves through branch $j$ and never returns. If $a=\sum a_j>0$, repeated returns restart the same probabilities by the <strong Markov property>. Thus the probability of $m$ returns is $(1-a)^m$, and eventual escape through $j$ is $\sum_{m\geq0}(1-a)^m a_j=a_j/a$. When the branches have outward random-walk drift, the final infinite excursion tends to the corresponding end almost surely.

= Discrete-time Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$\mathbb P(Y_{n+1}=j\mid\mathcal F_n)=P_{Y_nj}$}

A time-homogeneous discrete-time <Markov chain> has transitions at <integer> times with the displayed conditional <probabilities> and a row-stochastic <transition probability matrix> $P$. A <probability> row vector $\pi$ is a <stationary distribution> when $\pi P=\pi$. It is <reversible> when $\pi_iP_{ij}=\pi_jP_{ji}$; multiplying these equations along a finite path proves equality of its forward and reversed stationary path <probabilities>.

= Discrete-time Markov chain martingale characterization
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$M_n^f=f(X_n)-f(X_0)-\sum_{j<n}(Pf-f)(X_j)$}

An adapted process on a finite or countable state space has the <Markov chain> transition matrix $P$ exactly when the displayed process is a <martingale> for every bounded test function $f$. The forward implication follows by taking the <conditional expectation> of its increment. Conversely, the martingale property gives $\mathbb E[f(X_{n+1})\mid\mathcal F_n]=(Pf)(X_n)$; testing individual-state indicators recovers the entire conditional transition law. Stopping these martingales turns equations $(P-I)h=0$ and $(P-I)v=-1$ into hitting probabilities and expected hitting times.

= Nonnegative superharmonic function for a Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$g\ge Pg\ge0$}

For a <transition matrix> $P$, a finite-valued function on its state space is nonnegative superharmonic when $g\ge Pg\ge0$. Thus $g\ge0$ and its expected value cannot increase after one step. Replacing transitions on a target set by identity rows preserves $g\ge\widetilde Pg$. Iterating this inequality gives $g\ge\widetilde P^{\,n}g$, a useful bound for <hitting probabilities> without a separate optional-stopping theorem.

= Recurrence characterized by constant nonnegative superharmonic functions
{parent=Nonnegative superharmonic function for a Markov chain}

An irreducible <Markov chain> is recurrent exactly when every <nonnegative superharmonic function for a Markov chain> is constant. In the recurrent case, absorb the chain at $j$. The inequality $g_i\ge(\widetilde P^{\,n}g)_i\ge g_j\mathbb P_i(\tau_j\le n)$ and almost-sure hitting give $g_i\ge g_j$; exchange $i,j$. In the transient case, the <hitting probability> of a state $j$ is one there and satisfies the superharmonic inequality, but its one-step average at $j$ is the strictly smaller return probability. It is a nonconstant example.

= Markov chain invariant measure
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$xP=x$}
{c}

A nonnegative row vector $x$, not necessarily of total mass one, is an invariant measure for a <Markov chain> if $x_j=\sum_i x_ip_{ij}$. On an <irreducible Markov chain>, every nonzero invariant measure has positive coordinates: iterate stationarity and use a positive transition probability along a path.

= Excursion occupation measure
{parent=Markov chain invariant measure}
{title2=$\gamma_j^k=\mathbb E_k\sum_{n=0}^{T_k-1}\mathbf1_{\{X_n=j\}}$}

For a <recurrent Markov chain>, start at $k$ and stop at its first positive return $T_k$. The expected visits to each state before that return form the excursion occupation measure. Its $k$th coordinate is one. Counting the return endpoint instead of the start gives the same measure. It is invariant by shifting the occupation sum one time step and using the <Markov property>. Any <Markov chain invariant measure> normalized by $x_k=1$ dominates it: iterate the stationarity equations on the state space with $k$ removed, then retain the nonnegative excursion-path terms.

= Geometric-jump countdown Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$p_{i,i-1}=1,\quad p_{0j}=pq^{j-1}$}

On the nonnegative integers, every positive state decreases by one, while zero jumps to $j\geq1$ with probability $pq^{j-1}$, $p=1-q\in(0,1)$. Its return time from zero is one plus this geometric jump, so it has mean $(1+p)/p$. Return lengths two and three occur, establishing <aperiodicity>; deterministic downward motion and positive jumps establish irreducibility.

= Stationary distribution of a regenerative countdown chain
{parent=Geometric-jump countdown Markov chain}
{title2=$\pi_j=pq^{j-1}/(1+p),\quad j\geq1$}

For the <geometric-jump countdown Markov chain>, regeneration at zero gives
$$
\pi_0=\frac p{1+p},\qquad \pi_j=\frac p{1+p}q^{j-1}\quad(j\geq1).
$$
A cycle visits positive state $j$ precisely when its jump reaches at least $j$, with probability $q^{j-1}$. Dividing this expected occupation by the mean cycle length proves the formula. Mean recurrence times are $1/\pi_j$, and the limiting probability away from zero is $1/(1+p)$.

= Strong lumpability
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$\sum_{j\in C}p_{ij}=\sum_{j\in C}p_{i\prime j}$}

A partition of a <Markov chain> state space is strongly lumpable when, for every target block $C$, the sum $\sum_{j\in C}p_{ij}$ is identical for all source states $i$ in a given source block. These sums define the <transition matrix> of the block-valued <Markov chain>, independently of the initial law. Failure of this condition means the projection is not guaranteed to be a homogeneous Markov chain for every initial law, although special initial laws may still produce a Markov process.

= Harris recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}

= Harris recurrence
{c}
{synonym}

A <phi-irreducible> <Markov chain> is Harris recurrent when every set of positive irreducibility measure is hit almost surely from every state. With an invariant probability, it becomes a <positive Harris recurrent Markov chain>.

= Discrete-time multi-state model
{parent=Markov chain}

A discrete-time model describes individual transitions between states at successive steps. State occupancy counts, first-entry counts and transition counts are distinct quantities, especially when a state has a self-loop. With independent individuals and fixed transition probabilities, destination counts conditional on the source count have a <multinomial distribution>.

= Discrete-time multi-state diagnosis model
{parent=Discrete-time multi-state model}
{title2=$e_k=ah_{k-1}$}

With a one-step early state, early diagnosis probability $a$, and non-early diagnosis probability $b$ per subsequent step, mean infections $h_i$ yield early diagnoses $e_k=ah_{k-1}$ and late diagnoses $n_k=b(1-a)\sum_{i\leq k-2}h_i(1-b)^{k-i-2}$. The early intensity fraction is $e_k/(e_k+n_k)$ when positive. Initial infections and endpoint conventions determine the index limits.

= Positive Harris recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}

A Harris recurrent chain visits every measurable set of positive irreducibility measure almost surely. Positive Harris recurrence additionally supplies an invariant probability measure. The <ergodic theorem for a positive Harris recurrent Markov chain> gives consistency of time averages of integrable functions.

= Ergodic theorem for a positive Harris recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Positive Harris recurrent Markov chain}

For a positive Harris recurrent <Markov chain> with invariant probability $\pi$ and $\int|h|\,d\pi<\infty$, its time average converges almost surely to the invariant expectation:
$$
\frac1N\sum_{t=1}^Nh(X_t)\longrightarrow\int h\,d\pi.
$$
This is the consistency result for <Markov chain Monte Carlo> averages. Independent-observation variance formulas do not follow from this theorem.

= Ehrenfest urn model
{c}
{parent=Markov chain}
{title2=$P_{i,i+1}=(k-i)/k,\quad P_{i,i-1}=i/k$}

The Ehrenfest urn model moves one uniformly chosen ball between two boxes at each step. For $k\geq1$ balls, the count in one box is a <Markov chain> on $\{0,\ldots,k\}$. Its <stationary distribution> is $2^{-k}\binom ki$, and <detailed balance> makes it a <reversible Markov chain>. The chain has period two, but its <mean recurrence time> to the all-in-one-box state is still $2^k$.

= Random transposition shuffle
{parent=Markov chain}

Choose two positions independently and uniformly from $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ and swap them. This <Markov chain> on the <symmetric group> holds with probability $1/n$ and assigns probability $2/n^2$ to each unordered <transposition>. Its <stationary distribution> is uniform, and its ordinary <spectral gap> is $2/n$ for $n\geq2$. The latter follows from the <Aldous spectral gap theorem>: the single-label <continuous-time Markov chain> has rate $2/n^2$ between every pair of positions, whose mean-zero <eigenvalues> are all $-2/n$.

= Separation chain for two labels in a circular transposition shuffle
{parent=Random transposition shuffle}

In a <random transposition shuffle> on a six-cycle, the shorter circular distance between two distinguished labels takes values $1,2,3$. The symmetry of the cycle and exchangeability of other labels make this a <lumped Markov chain>. Counting the fifteen possible transpositions gives <transition matrix> $P=\frac1{15}\begin{pmatrix}9&4&2\\4&9&2\\4&4&7\end{pmatrix}$. Its <stationary distribution> is $(2/5,2/5,1/5)$ and $P=\frac13I+\frac23\Pi$, where each row of $\Pi$ is this <stationary distribution>. Consequently the probability of opposite labels after $n$ steps from adjacent labels is $(1-3^{-n})/5$.

= Random adjacent transposition shuffle
{parent=Markov chain}

On the <symmetric group> $S_n$, this <Markov chain> chooses uniformly from the identity and the $n-1$ adjacent <transpositions>, and multiplies on the right. Its <stationary distribution> is the <uniform distribution on a finite set>, because the generators are self-inverse. A fixed label's position is a <lumped Markov chain> on a <path graph> with off-diagonal <transition probabilities> $1/n$ to existing neighbours.

= Spectral gap of the adjacent transposition shuffle
{parent=Random adjacent transposition shuffle}
{title2=$\gamma=2(1-\cos(\pi/n))/n$}

For the <random adjacent transposition shuffle> with $n\geq2$, the generator $P-I$ is the <interchange process> on a <path graph> with each edge rate $1/n$. The <Aldous spectral gap theorem> reduces its <spectral gap> to that of the single-label <random walk>. The <eigenfunctions> $k\mapsto\cos(\ell\pi(k-1/2)/n)$, for $0\leq\ell<n$, have generator <eigenvalues> $-2(1-\cos(\ell\pi/n))/n$; direct substitution verifies both the interior and endpoint equations. Consequently $\gamma\sim\pi^2/n^3$.

= Tagged-label Rayleigh quotient for adjacent transpositions
{parent=Random adjacent transposition shuffle}
{title2=$\gamma\leq12/[n^2(n+1)]$}

For the <random adjacent transposition shuffle> on $S_n$ with $n\geq2$, the position $f(\sigma)=\sigma^{-1}(i)$ of one label is uniform on $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ in equilibrium, so $\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)=(n^2-1)/12$. Each adjacent <transposition> moves the label with probability $2/n$, giving $\mathcal E(f,f)=(n-1)/n^2$. The <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> variational formula therefore gives $\gamma\leq12/[n^2(n+1)]$. In particular the continuous <uniform distribution> on $(0,1)$ has <variance> $1/12$, not $1/6$.

= Absorbing state
{parent=Markov chain}

An absorbing state of a Markov chain has transition probability one to itself. Once the chain enters it, the chain never leaves.

= Markov kernel
{c}
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Transition kernel
{synonym}

A Markov kernel $K(x,\mathord\cdot)$ assigns a <probability distribution> to each current state $x$ and acts on a distribution $P$ by $(PK)(A)=\int K(x,A)P(dx)$.

= Birth-death chain
{parent=Markov chain}

A birth-death chain is a discrete-time <Markov chain> on nonnegative integers that can move only to the same state or a neighbouring state.

= Excursion weights of a reflected birth-death chain
{parent=Birth-death chain}
{title2=$w_n=\prod_{r=1}^n p_{r-1}/q_r$}

For a nearest-neighbour chain on the nonnegative integers, reflect at zero with holding probability $q_0$ and upward probability $p_0$. In a recurrent excursion from zero until its first return, expected visits $w_i$ satisfy $w_0=1$ and $w_ip_i=w_{i+1}q_{i+1}$, because each upward crossing is paired with a downward crossing. Thus the expected excursion length is $\sum_iw_i$ by <Tonelli's theorem>. Positive recurrence is equivalent to finiteness of that sum. The hitting-probability scale weights are $\rho_n=\prod_{r=1}^nq_r/p_r$, and $w_n\rho_n=p_0/p_n$ helps show that summable excursion weights imply recurrence.

= Product Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}

Independent Markov chains with transition matrices $P$ and $Q$ form a product chain with transition matrix $P\otimes Q$. If their stationary distributions are $\pi$ and $\rho$, the product distribution $\pi\otimes\rho$ is stationary.

= Stochastic matrix
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Transition matrix
{synonym}

The transition matrix of a finite <Markov chain> has entries
$$
P_{ij}=\mathbb P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_n=i).
$$

= Transition probability
{title2=$P_{ij}$}
{parent=Stochastic matrix}

The transition probability $P_{ij}$ is the conditional probability that a <Markov chain> moves to state $j$ on its next step given that its current state is $i$.

= Irreducible Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A <Markov chain> is irreducible when every state can reach every other state along a path of positive-probability transitions.

= Phi-irreducibility
{parent=Irreducible Markov chain}
{title2=$\varphi(A)>0\Longrightarrow\sum_{n\geq1}P^n(x,A)>0$}

= Phi-irreducible
{synonym}

A <Markov chain> with transition kernel $P$ is $\varphi$-irreducible if $\varphi$ is a nonzero <sigma-finite measure> and every measurable set $A$ with $\varphi(A)>0$ satisfies $\sum_{n\geq1}P^n(x,A)>0$ from every starting state $x$. This is positive reachability, not certain eventual hitting.

= Aperiodic Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}

= Aperiodicity
{synonym}

= Aperiodic
{synonym}

A state is aperiodic when the greatest common divisor of its positive-probability return times is one. A <Markov chain> is aperiodic when all its states are aperiodic.

= Recurrent state
{parent=Markov chain}

A state is recurrent when a <Markov chain> started there returns to it with probability one.

= Transient state
{parent=Markov chain}

A state is transient when its probability of ever returning is less than one.

This is the alternative to a <recurrent state> for the return behavior of a <Markov chain>.

= First return time
{parent=Markov chain}

The first return time to a state $i$ is
$$
T_i^+=\inf\{n\geq1:X_n=i\}.
$$

= Mean recurrence time
{parent=First return time}

The mean recurrence time of $i$ is $\mathbb E_iT_i^+$. In a finite irreducible <Markov chain> with <stationary distribution> $\pi$, it equals $1/\pi_i$.

= Preservation under enlargement of transition support
{parent=Markov chain}

If two finite <Markov chains> satisfy
$$
P_{ij}>0\Longrightarrow Q_{ij}>0,
$$
then every positive-probability path and return cycle for $P$ also exists for $Q$. Consequently irreducibility and aperiodicity pass from $P$ to $Q$. Recurrence and mean return time need not do so, because the added transitions and changed probabilities can create escape routes or make returns less frequent.

= Running minimum of an independent sequence is Markov
{parent=Markov chain}

For <independent random variables> with a common law, $L_n=\min_{j\leq n}X_j$ satisfies $L_{n+1}=\min(L_n,X_{n+1})$. The next-state law therefore depends on the past only through $L_n$.

= Overlapping moving sum need not be Markov
{parent=Markov chain}

For i.i.d. Bernoulli variables, $K_n=X_{n-1}+X_n$ hides which summand is the newest one. Given $K_n=1$, the previous value can reveal $X_n$ and thereby change the conditional law of $K_{n+1}$, so $(K_n)$ need not be a Markov chain.

= Reversible Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Reversible
{synonym}

A stationary Markov chain $(X_t:0\leq t\leq T)$ is reversible in equilibrium when it has the same finite-dimensional distributions as its time reversal $(X_{T-t}:0\leq t\leq T)$. Equivalently, its stationary flow satisfies detailed balance.

= Weighted matching Markov chain
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}
{title2=$\pi_\lambda(M)=\lambda^{|M|}/Z_G(\lambda)$}

On the <matchings in a graph>, a <lazy Markov chain> proposes adding an <edge>, deleting an <edge>, or exchanging an <edge> for the unique matching <edge> incident with it. A proposal incident with two matching <edges> is rejected. Select the proposed <edge> uniformly and use the <Metropolis–Hastings acceptance probability> for the displayed weights. Symmetric proposals give <detailed balance>; deletions connect every state to the empty <matching in a graph>. The normalizing constant is the <matching generating function>.

= Canonical paths for the weighted matching chain
{parent=Weighted matching Markov chain}
{title2=$\rho\leq16nm(m+1)^2\max(1,\lambda^2)\max(\lambda,\lambda^{-1})$}

Here the <graph> has $2n$ <vertices> and $m\geq1$ <edges>. The bound is a deliberately loose <canonical paths Poincare bound>, sufficient for polynomial <mixing time> when both activity and reciprocal activity are polynomially bounded. Order the alternating <graph paths> and cycles of $I\triangle F$ canonically. Convert each component from initial <matching in a graph> $I$ to final <matching in a graph> $F$ by moving an unmatched endpoint using exchanges; open a cycle with one deletion and finish it with one addition.

At an intermediate <matching in a graph> $M$, the complementary encoding $K=I\triangle F\triangle M$ is a <matching in a graph> away from the active component and has at most two degree-two <vertices>. Remove at most two <edges> to obtain a <matching in a graph> $K'$. Record those <edges> and one bit selecting the initial alternating colour on the active component. For a fixed directed transition this data reconstructs $K$, $I\triangle F$, the active component and its ordering, and then $I,F$; common <edges> are $M\cap K$. There are at most $2(m+1)^2$ auxiliary records per $K'$.

If $d=|K|-|K'|\leq2$, then $\pi_\lambda(I)\pi_\lambda(F)=\lambda^d\pi_\lambda(M)\pi_\lambda(K')$. The transition capacity is at least $\pi_\lambda(M)/(2m\max(\lambda,\lambda^{-1}))$, and the <path> length is at most $4n$. Summing over the injective encodings proves the displayed <path congestion> bound.

= Ordered-state visit times in a reversible chain
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}

Under a stationary initial law, reversal leaves the law of a finite reversible-chain path unchanged. The first time an ordered occurrence of state $a$ then state $b$ appears therefore has the same distribution as the reverse ordered occurrence. Decomposition at the first entrance relates this symmetry to differences of mean hitting times. If positive diagonal return times are used instead of zero diagonal entrance times, their stationary-weighted terms cancel by <Kac's lemma>.

= Reversible density-ratio propagation
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}
{title2=$d(K^t\nu)/d\pi=K^t(d\nu/d\pi)$}

For a <reversible Markov chain> with <stationary distribution> $\pi$ and $\nu\ll\pi$, <detailed balance> gives
$$
\frac{d(K^t\nu)}{d\pi}(x)=\int K^t(x,dy)\frac{d\nu}{d\pi}(y).
$$
To prove this, integrate either side against a bounded test <function> and exchange the two endpoints using <detailed balance>. If $\pi=h/Z$, the output <probability density function> is $q_t(x)=h(x)\mathbb E_{B\sim K^t(x,\cdot)}[\nu(B)/h(B)]$. A reverse simulation can therefore estimate this <density ratio> without evaluating a transition <probability density function>.

= Spectral kernel expansion of a reversible chain
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}
{title2=$P^t(x,y)/\pi(y)$}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> satisfying <detailed balance> with $\pi$, its <transition matrix> is a <self-adjoint operator> for $\langle f,g\rangle_\pi=\sum_x\pi(x)f(x)g(x)$. For a real <orthonormal eigenbasis> $(f_j)$ with <eigenvalues> $(\lambda_j)$,
$$
\frac{P^t(x,y)}{\pi(y)}=\sum_j\lambda_j^t f_j(x)f_j(y).
$$
Indeed, apply the <spectral decomposition> of $P^t$ to the function $\mathbf1_{\{y\}}/\pi(y)$, whose coefficient against $f_j$ is $f_j(y)$. For $t=0$, every <eigenvalue> multiplier is interpreted as one.

= Detailed balance
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Detailed balance equations
{synonym}

Detailed balance is the identity pi_i P_ij = pi_j P_ji for every pair of states.

= Detailed balance for a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Detailed balance}

For a <continuous-time Markov chain> with transition rates $q_{ij}$, detailed balance is
$$
\pi_iq_{ij}=\pi_jq_{ji}
$$
for every pair of distinct states. A probability distribution satisfying these equations is an <invariant distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain>, because summing over incoming rates gives $\pi Q=0$. Under the standard nonexplosion assumptions, the equilibrium chain is reversible.

= Dirichlet form of a Markov chain
{title2=$\mathcal E(f,g)$}
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}

For a reversible transition matrix $P$ with invariant distribution $\pi$,
$$
\mathcal E(f,g)=\frac12\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)P(x,y)
(f(x)-f(y))(g(x)-g(y)).
$$

This chain energy is a discrete example of a <Dirichlet form>.

= Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet form of a Markov chain}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq C\mathcal E(f,f)$}

= Discrete Poincare inequality
{c}
{synonym}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> with at least two states satisfying <detailed balance>, its <Dirichlet form of a Markov chain> obeys
$$
\gamma=1-\lambda_2=\inf_{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)>0}\frac{\mathcal E(f,f)}{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)}.
$$
Expand $f-\pi(f)$ in an <orthonormal eigenbasis>. The denominator is $\sum_{j\geq2}a_j^2$, and the numerator is $\sum_{j\geq2}(1-\lambda_j)a_j^2$. Their ratio is at least $1-\lambda_2$ and equality holds for the second <eigenfunction>. Thus the optimal <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> constant is $1/\gamma$, for the ordinary rather than absolute <spectral gap>.

= Discrete Dirichlet energy
{title2=$\mathcal E(f,f)$}
{parent=Dirichlet form of a Markov chain}

On an unweighted graph, the discrete Dirichlet energy is proportional to $\sum_{xy\in E}(f(x)-f(y))^2$. It measures the squared variation of a function across edges.

= Canonical paths comparison theorem
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet form of a Markov chain}

The canonical paths comparison theorem compares two reversible chains by routing every transition of one chain along a path of transitions of the other. The maximum capacity-normalized path congestion bounds the ratio of their Dirichlet forms and hence their spectral gaps.

= Canonical paths Poincare bound
{parent=Canonical paths comparison theorem}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq\rho\mathcal E(f,f)$}

Choose a directed <path> $\eta_{xy}$ of positive-probability transitions for each ordered pair of states in a finite <reversible Markov chain>. Write $Q(e)=\pi(u)P(u,v)$ for a directed <edge> $e=(u,v)$, and let $N_e(\eta)$ count its occurrences. Define
$$
\rho=\max_{e:Q(e)>0}\frac1{Q(e)}\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)\pi(y)|\eta_{xy}|N_e(\eta_{xy}).
$$
The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> bounds $(f(x)-f(y))^2$ by $|\eta_{xy}|$ times the sum of squared differences along the <path>. Multiply by $\pi(x)\pi(y)/2$, sum and interchange finite sums. The identity $\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)=\frac12\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)\pi(y)(f(x)-f(y))^2$ proves the bound. This convention counts directed <edges> and retains the factor $1/2$ in the <Dirichlet form of a Markov chain>.

= Path congestion
{parent=Canonical paths Poincare bound}
{title2=$\rho$}

For a family of transition <graph paths>, path congestion is the largest, over transitions, ratio of routed pair weight times <path> length to stationary transition capacity. The <canonical paths Poincare bound> makes this an upper bound on the <relaxation time>.

= Dirichlet energy space with zero boundary at infinity
{title2=$H_0$}
{parent=Dirichlet form of a Markov chain}

For a transient weighted graph, $H_0$ is the completion of the finitely supported functions in the norm $\lVert f\rVert_{H_0}=\mathcal E(f,f)^{1/2}$. Its elements have finite <Dirichlet form of a Markov chain>[Dirichlet energy] and vanish at infinity in the energy sense.

= Relaxation time
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{rel}}$}
{parent=Dirichlet form of a Markov chain}

The relaxation time of a finite reversible lazy <Markov chain> is the reciprocal of its <spectral gap>: $t_{\mathrm{rel}}=(1-\lambda_2)^{-1}$.

= Absolute relaxation time
{parent=Relaxation time}
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{rel},*}=1/\gamma_*$}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> with at least two states that is an <aperiodic Markov chain> and a <reversible Markov chain>, put $\gamma_*=1-\max_{j\geq2}|\lambda_j|$ and $t_{\mathrm{rel},*}=1/\gamma_*$. This is the reciprocal of the <absolute L2 spectral gap of a reversible Markov chain>. It agrees with the reciprocal of the ordinary <spectral gap> $1-\lambda_2$ for a <lazy Markov chain>, but need not agree when there are negative <eigenvalues>.

= Spectral profile
{title2=$\Lambda(r)$}
{parent=Relaxation time}

The spectral profile minimizes a Dirichlet-form Rayleigh quotient over nonnegative functions supported on sets of stationary mass at most $r$.

= Generalized Cheeger inequality
{c}
{parent=Spectral profile}

For the isoperimetric profile $\Phi_*(r)$ of a reversible chain and $r\leq1/2$,
$$
\frac{\Phi_*(r)^2}{2}\leq\Lambda(r)\leq\frac{\Phi_*(r)}{1-r}.
$$

= Conductance of a Markov chain
{title2=$\Phi(A)$}
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}

= Bottleneck ratio
{synonym}

For a <reversible Markov chain> with stationary flow $Q(x,y)=\pi(x)P(x,y)$, the conductance of a set $A$ is
$$
\Phi(A)=\frac{Q(A,A^c)}{\pi(A)}.
$$
Its <Cheeger constant> minimizes the boundary flow divided by the smaller stationary mass of the two sides of the cut.

= Cheeger constant
{c}
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Reversible Markov chain}

The Cheeger constant of a finite reversible chain is the least stationary boundary flow divided by the smaller stationary mass of a set and its complement.

= Cheeger inequality
{c}
{parent=Cheeger constant}

For a reversible chain with spectral gap $\gamma$ and Cheeger constant $\Phi$,
$$
\frac{\Phi^2}{2}\leq\gamma\leq2\Phi.
$$

= Mixing time of a Markov chain
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{mix}}(\varepsilon)$}
{parent=Markov chain}

= Mixing time
{synonym}

The $\varepsilon$-mixing time is the least $t$ for which every initial state has total variation distance at most $\varepsilon$ from the invariant distribution after $t$ steps.

= Eigenvalue lower bound for total variation mixing
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}
{title2=$d(t)\geq r^t/2$}

If $r=\max_{j\geq2}|\lambda_j|$ for a finite <irreducible Markov chain> with at least two states, then its worst-case <total variation distance> from its <stationary distribution> satisfies $d(t)\geq r^t/2$. Choose a possibly complex <eigenfunction> $f$ for an <eigenvalue> of modulus $r$, normalize $\max_x|f(x)|=1$, and start where the maximum is attained. Since $\pi(f)=0$, $|P^tf(x)-\pi(f)|=r^t$; the defining sum for <total variation distance> bounds this difference by $2d(t)$. For $0<r<1$, this yields $t_{\mathrm{mix}}(\varepsilon)\geq(r/(1-r))\log(1/(2\varepsilon))$ for $0<\varepsilon<1/2$, because $-r\log r\leq1-r$.

= Pairwise mixing diameter
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}
{title2=$\bar d(t)$}

For a finite <Markov chain> with <transition matrix> $P$, define $\bar d(t)=\max_{x,y}\|P^t(x,\cdot)-P^t(y,\cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}$. If $\pi$ is a <stationary distribution> and $d(t)=\max_x\|P^t(x,\cdot)-\pi\|_{\mathrm{TV}}$, then $d(t)\leq\bar d(t)\leq2d(t)$. The first bound follows by writing $\pi=\sum_y\pi(y)P^t(y,\cdot)$ and using the <triangle inequality>; the second uses the <triangle inequality> through $\pi$.

= Strong stationary time
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}

A randomized <stopping time> $\tau$ is a strong stationary time for a <Markov chain> with <stationary distribution> $\pi$ when $X_\tau$ has distribution $\pi$ and is <independent random variables>[independent] of $\tau$. Equivalently,
$$
\mathbb P_x(X_\tau=y,\tau=t)=\pi(y)\mathbb P_x(\tau=t).
$$

= Separation distance
{title2=$s_x(t)$}
{parent=Strong stationary time}

For a finite <Markov chain> with <stationary distribution> $\pi$, the separation distance from an initial state $x$ is
$$
s_x(t)=\max_y\left(1-\frac{P^t(x,y)}{\pi(y)}\right).
$$
If $\tau$ is a <strong stationary time>, then $s_x(t)\leq\mathbb P_x(\tau>t)$, and <total variation distance> is at most separation distance.

= Spectral upper bound for total variation mixing
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}

For a finite transitive reversible chain with nontrivial eigenvalues $\lambda_i$,
$$
4\lVert P^t(x,\cdot)-\pi\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}^2
\leq\sum_{i\geq2}\lambda_i^{2t}.
$$

= Cutoff for Markov chains
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}

A sequence of chains exhibits cutoff when its distance from stationarity drops from near one to near zero in a time window negligible compared with its mixing-time scale.

= Ratio definition of cutoff
{parent=Cutoff for Markov chains}

The ratio convention for <cutoff for Markov chains> requires $t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\varepsilon)/t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(1-\varepsilon)\to1$ for every fixed $0<\varepsilon<1/2$, with positive denominators eventually. Equivalently, any two fixed levels have asymptotically equal <mixing times>. This convention permits a bounded-time jump; definitions which additionally require diverging <mixing times> exclude such a jump. For nonlazy <simple random walk> on the <complete graph> $K_n$, every fixed-level <mixing time> is eventually one.

= Peres product condition
{c}
{parent=Ratio definition of cutoff}
{title2=$\gamma_n t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}\longrightarrow\infty$}

For finite <irreducible Markov chains> that are <aperiodic Markov chains> and <reversible Markov chains>, the <ratio definition of cutoff> and $T_n=t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(1/4)\to\infty$ imply the Peres product condition, with $\gamma_n=1-\lambda_{2,n}$. If $\gamma_nT_n\leq B$ on a subsequence, then $\gamma_{*,n}\leq\gamma_n$ and the <eigenvalue lower bound for total variation mixing> give
$$
\frac{t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\varepsilon)}{T_n}\geq\left(\frac1B-\frac1{T_n}\right)\log\frac1{2\varepsilon}.
$$
For sufficiently small fixed $\varepsilon$, this contradicts the limit one forced by <cutoff for Markov chains>. The same argument proves the stronger conclusion $\gamma_{*,n}T_n\to\infty$.

= Random-to-top card shuffle
{parent=Cutoff for Markov chains}

The random-to-top shuffle chooses one uniformly random card and moves it to the top of the deck. The time at which every card has been chosen is a <strong stationary time>, yielding cutoff at $n\log n$ with a window of order $n$.

= Top-to-random card shuffle
{parent=Random-to-top card shuffle}

The top-to-random shuffle removes the top card and inserts it in a uniformly random position. Under the uniform measure on permutations it is the time reversal of the <random-to-top card shuffle>, so the two shuffles have the same total-variation mixing profile.

= Pre-cutoff for Markov chains
{parent=Cutoff for Markov chains}

A sequence of chains exhibits pre-cutoff if there are constants $0<A<B<\infty$ and times $t_n$ such that the worst-case distance tends to one at $At_n$ and to zero at $Bt_n$. For reversible chains, the product condition $t_{\mathrm{rel}}=o(t_{\mathrm{mix}})$ is necessary for pre-cutoff.

= Stationary-reset perturbation of a Markov chain
{parent=Cutoff for Markov chains}

For $\widetilde P=(1-a)P+a\Pi$, where every row of $\Pi$ equals the stationary distribution $\pi$,
$$
\widetilde P^t-\Pi=(1-a)^t(P^t-\Pi).
$$
The perturbation superposes gradual exponential mixing on the original profile and can destroy pre-cutoff.

= Shift-refresh chain on a hypercube
{parent=Cutoff for Markov chains}

A shift-refresh chain cyclically moves a pointer through the coordinates of a bit vector and replaces the visited bit by an independent fair bit. Once the pointer walk has visited every residue, all coordinates have been refreshed, giving a <strong stationary time>.

= Path coupling theorem
{parent=Mixing time of a Markov chain}
{c}

If adjacent states admit one-step couplings that contract a path metric by a common factor, then the induced Wasserstein distance contracts by the same factor for arbitrary starting distributions.

= Noisy voter model
{parent=Markov chain}

The noisy voter model updates a chosen vertex either by replacing its spin with fresh random noise or by copying a neighboring spin. A positive refresh probability destroys conserved opinions and permits contractive coupling bounds even on nonregular graphs.

= Lumped Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}

A lumped Markov chain records only a partition class of the original state. It is Markov when the transition probability into every class is constant within each source class.

= Uniform conditional law for a triangular-wedge walk
{parent=Lumped Markov chain}

For a triangular-lattice walk in a sixty-degree wedge with rate $1/6$ on interior edges and doubled outward boundary rates, the horizontal position is conditionally uniform on $\{0,\ldots,k\}$ given its current vertical level and past levels. Within level $k$, the reflecting horizontal generator is symmetric, while vertical killing has constant rate $2/3$, so the uniform law survives any holding period. Each column of the upward or downward transition-rate block has sum $1/3$ for $k>0$, yielding a uniform conditional law at the next level. The induced <birth-death process> has rates $\lambda_k=(k+2)/(3(k+1))$ and $\mu_k=k/(3(k+1))$. Its probability of ever hitting zero from level $k$ is $1/(k+1)$, so it is transient.

= Hitting probability
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A hitting probability is the probability of reaching one set before another and solves a discrete harmonic boundary problem.

= Maximum occupation is not a hitting probability
{parent=Hitting probability}
{title2=$\sup_{t>0}\mathbb P(Y_t>\ell)\ne\mathbb P(\sup_{t\geq0}Y_t\geq\ell)$}

For $Y_t=-vt+\sqrt{2D}\,B_t$ with $v,D,\ell>0$ and standard <Brownian motion>, the one-time occupation probability is $\tfrac12\operatorname{erfc}[(\ell+vt)/\sqrt{4Dt}]$. It is maximized at $t=\ell/v$, giving $\tfrac12\operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{v\ell/D})$. The actual <hitting probability> is $e^{-v\ell/D}$. To derive it, solve $Dh''-vh'=0$ on $(-A,\ell)$ with $h(-A)=0$, $h(\ell)=1$, obtaining $h(0)=(1-e^{-vA/D})/(e^{v\ell/D}-e^{-vA/D})$, and let $A\to\infty$. A path that reached the level and returned contributes to the latter event but not to a given one-time tail.

= Tournament won by two consecutive victories
{parent=Hitting probability}

With three evenly matched players, the previous winner plays the previous spectator. A pair of successive distinct winners $ij$ moves to $jj$ or $jk$ with equal probabilities, where $k$ is the remaining player. The transient states form two directed 3-cycles and each step has absorption probability one half. Starting with a game between two specified players, their overall winning probabilities are each $5/14$, while the original spectator wins with probability $2/7$.

= Hitting probability is the minimal nonnegative harmonic extension
{parent=Hitting probability}
{title2=$h_i^A=\mathbb P_i(H^A<\infty)$}

The <hitting probability> of a set is one on that set and transition-harmonic elsewhere by the <Markov property>. Finite-horizon hitting probabilities begin at the boundary indicator and increase through the same positive transition recursion. Any nonnegative solution dominates every iterate; monotone passage to infinite time shows that it dominates the hitting probability. This proves minimality without requiring almost-sure hitting.

= Splitting probability
{parent=Hitting probability}

= Absorption probability
{synonym}

A splitting probability is the probability that a stochastic process reaches one target before competing targets. For a diffusion it is harmonic for the <Kolmogorov backward equation>, with boundary values one on the selected target and zero on the others.

= First-passage time
{title2=$T_A$}
{parent=Hitting probability}

= Hitting time
{synonym}

The first-passage time to a target set $A$ is $T_A=\inf\{t\geq0:X_t\in A\}$. It is also called a <hitting time>.

= Brownian first-passage time
{c}
{parent=First-passage time}
{title2=$T_a$}

For standard <Brownian motion> and $a>0$, $T_a=\inf\{t\geq0:B_t\geq a\}$ is finite <almost surely> and
$$
\mathbb P(T_a\leq t)=2\left(1-\Phi\left(\frac a{\sqrt t}\right)\right),\qquad t>0.
$$
The <Brownian reflection principle> gives this <distribution function>. The <Brownian scaling> identity $T_{ca}\overset d=c^2T_a$ and the <strong Markov property> show its <probability distribution> is a <strictly stable distribution> with index $1/2$.

= Exponential terminal moment below a linear Brownian boundary
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}
{title2=$\mathbb E[e^{\theta W_t}\mathbf1_{\{T_{a,b}>t\}}]$}

For $T_{a,b}=\inf\{s:W_s=a+bs\}$ with $a>0$, exponential tilting of the terminal Brownian value gives $e^{\theta^2t/2}$ times the barrier-survival probability for Brownian drift $\theta-b$. Thus the moment equals $e^{\theta^2t/2}[\Phi((a+(b-\theta)t)/\sqrt t)-e^{2a(\theta-b)}\Phi((-a+(b-\theta)t)/\sqrt t)]$ for every real $\theta$.

= Inverse-square Gaussian law of Brownian first passage
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}
{title2=$\tau_x\overset d=x^2/N^2$}

The <Brownian reflection principle> gives $\mathbb P(\tau_x\leq t)=\mathbb P(|N|\geq x/\sqrt t)$ for a standard normal random variable $N$. This is the distribution function of $x^2/N^2$. An independent <Brownian motion> observed at this hitting time has the <Cauchy distribution> with scale $x$.

= Drifted Brownian first-passage density
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}
{title2=$h_c(t)=ae^{-(a-ct)^2/(2t)}/\sqrt{2\pi t^3}$}

For a positive level $a$, this density describes the <first-passage time> of $B_t+ct$. The <Girsanov theorem> multiplies the zero-drift density by the stopped likelihood $e^{ca-c^2t/2}$. Its total mass is one for nonnegative drift and $e^{2ac}$ for negative drift; the latter has remaining mass at infinite hitting time.

= Linear-boundary Brownian first-passage distribution
{parent=Drifted Brownian first-passage density}
{title2=$T_{a,b}=\inf\{t:B_t=a+bt\}$}

For $a>0$, real $b$ and standard <Brownian motion>, the <first-passage time> to a linear boundary has
$$
\mathbb P(T_{a,b}\leq t)=e^{-2ab}\Phi((bt-a)/\sqrt t)+\Phi(-(a+bt)/\sqrt t),\qquad t>0.
$$
Here $\Phi$ is the <standard normal distribution function>. Differentiation gives $a\exp(-(a+bt)^2/(2t))/\sqrt{2\pi t^3}$, the <drifted Brownian first-passage density>. Its <Laplace transform> is $\exp[-a(b+\sqrt{b^2+2\theta})]$ for $\theta>0$. There is mass $1-e^{-2ab}$ at infinity when $b>0$; for $b\leq0$ the hitting time is finite <almost surely>.

= Truncated discounted Brownian first passage
{parent=Drifted Brownian first-passage density}
{title2=$\mathbb E[e^{-rH_a}\mathbf1\{H_a\leq T\}]$}

When $d=\sqrt{c^2+2r}$ is real and nonnegative, the discounted <drifted Brownian first-passage density> is $e^{a(c-d)}h_d(t)$. Integrating yields $e^{a(c-d)}\Phi((dT-a)/\sqrt T)+e^{a(c+d)}\Phi((-a-dT)/\sqrt T)$. In a <Black-Scholes model> with $c=(r-\sigma^2/2)/\sigma$, the identity $c^2+2r=(r+\sigma^2/2)^2/\sigma^2$ ensures this formula covers every real interest rate.

= First-passage Laplace transform for Brownian motion with drift
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}
{title2=$\mathbb E e^{-\lambda S}=e^{-y(a+\sqrt{a^2+2\lambda})}$}

For $Y_t=y+B_t+at$, $y>0$, and $S=\inf\{t\ge0:Y_t=0\}$,
$$
\mathbb E e^{-\lambda S}=\exp[-y(a+\sqrt{a^2+2\lambda})],\qquad\lambda>0,
$$
where $e^{-\lambda\infty}=0$. The <diffusion generator> is $\frac12\partial_{yy}+a\partial_y$. Its bounded eigenfunction on the positive half-line with boundary value one is $u(y)=\exp[-y(a+\sqrt{a^2+2\lambda})]$, giving the formula through the <discounted boundary-hitting representation>. The zero-discount limit is the hitting probability $e^{-2y\max(a,0)}$.

= Fixed-level versus simultaneous Brownian passage-time equality
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}

For standard <Brownian motion>, set $H_a=\inf\{t:B_t\geq a\}$ and $T_a=\inf\{t:B_t>a\}$. The <strong Markov property> and immediate positive excursions give $T_a=H_a$ <almost surely> for each fixed $a\geq0$. However, these level-indexed processes are not <indistinguishable stochastic processes>. <Almost surely> the random level $A=\sup_{t\leq1}B_t$ is positive and exceeds $B_1$. It is first attained before time one but is not exceeded until after time one, so $H_A<1<T_A$. The simultaneous-equality <event> therefore has probability zero. The strict passage process $T_a$ is the right-continuous choice needed for a <subordinator>.

= Brownian first-passage Laplace transform
{c}
{parent=Brownian first-passage time}
{title2=$\mathbb E e^{-\lambda H_a}=e^{-a\sqrt{2\lambda}}$}

For standard <Brownian motion> started at zero, $H_a=\inf\{t\geq0:B_t\geq a\}$ is finite <almost surely> for $a\geq0$ and
$$
\mathbb E e^{-\lambda H_a}=e^{-a\sqrt{2\lambda}},\qquad\lambda\geq0.
$$
The <Brownian reflection principle> proves finiteness. For $u\geq0$, the <exponential martingale for Brownian motion> stopped at $H_a\wedge t$ is bounded by $e^{ua}$. The <dominated convergence theorem> gives $\mathbb E e^{ua-u^2H_a/2}=1$, and taking $u=\sqrt{2\lambda}$ gives the transform.

= First-passage-time density
{title2=$f_\tau(t)$}
{parent=First-passage time}

When a <first-passage time> $\tau$ has an absolutely continuous distribution, its density is $f_\tau(t)=-S'(t)$, where $S(t)=\mathbb P(\tau>t)$ is its <survival function>. In a <continuous-time Markov chain>, this density is the total probability flux from surviving states into the absorbing set.

= Mean first-passage time
{title2=$\tau(x)=\mathbb E_xT_A$}
{parent=First-passage time}

For a diffusion with backward generator $L$, the mean first-passage time satisfies $L\tau=-1$ in the interior, with the backward forms of the process's boundary conditions.

= Transition-completion time in a Markov chain
{parent=Mean first-passage time}

The waiting time for a specified directed edge to be traversed differs from the waiting time to visit its terminal state. First-step equations include no continuation term when the designated transition succeeds, but still count that last step. They can also be derived by augmenting the chain with an absorbing success state.

= Survival probability
{title2=$Q(x,t)$}
{parent=First-passage time}

The survival probability is $Q(x,t)=\mathbb P_x(T_A>t)$. When absorption eventually occurs,
$$
\mathbb E_xT_A=\int_0^\infty Q(x,t)\,dt.
$$

= Harmonic hitting probability for the balanced-difference urn
{parent=Hitting probability}

Suppose an urn starts with $m$ green balls and $m+2$ red balls. Drawing green removes one green ball, while drawing red adds one green and one red ball. The difference remains two, and $h(g)=1/(g+1)$ is harmonic for the green-ball chain. Stopping $h$ on hitting $0$ or $N$ and then letting $N$ tend to infinity gives termination probability
$$
\mathbb P_m(\tau_0<\infty)=\frac1{m+1}.
$$

= Expected hitting time
{parent=Markov chain}

For a target set $A$, the expected hitting time $g(i)=\mathbb E_iT_A$ satisfies $g=0$ on $A$ and
$$
g(i)=1+\sum_jP_{ij}g(j)
$$
outside $A$, whenever the expectation is finite.

= Constant-drift hitting-time identity
{parent=Expected hitting time}
{title2=$\mathbb E_xT=f(x)/\delta$}

For a finite <Markov chain>, let $T$ be the first <hitting time> of $D$, assume $T<\infty$ <almost surely>, $f=0$ on $D$ and $Pf=f-\delta$ on its complement, with $\delta>0$. The <stopped martingale> $f(X_{n\wedge T})+\delta(n\wedge T)$ has mean $f(x)$. Boundedness of $f$ and <bounded convergence theorem> give $\mathbb E f(X_{n\wedge T})\to0$; <monotone convergence> then gives the stated finite <expected hitting time>. No drift condition is required after entry into $D$.

= Hitting a hub from two four-cycles
{parent=Expected hitting time}

Take two four-cycles, connect corresponding vertices, and connect each inner vertex to an absorbing hub. For the ordinary <random walk on a graph>, symmetry reduces mean remaining times to outer and inner types, satisfying $O=1+(2O+I)/3$ and $I=1+(O+2I)/4$. Thus $O=10$, $I=7$. For the <non-backtracking random walk>, directed-edge types outer-outer, outer-inner, inner-outer and inner-inner give remaining means $13/2,9/2,15/2,21/4$. Starting at an outer vertex, the first-step distribution yields total mean $41/6$.

= Hitting time of the bridge vertex between two cliques
{parent=Expected hitting time}
{title2=$h_1=n^2-n+1,\quad h_o=n^2$}

For <simple random walk> on two disjoint <complete graphs> of size $n\geq2$ joined through one new vertex $w$, let $h_1$ be the <expected hitting time> of $w$ from either vertex attached to $w$, and $h_o$ the <expected hitting time> from any other clique vertex. <First-step analysis> gives $h_1=1+(n-1)h_o/n$ and $h_o=h_1+n-1$, so $h_1=n^2-n+1$ and $h_o=n^2$. The <expected hitting time> from $w$ is zero. In discrete time the <random walk> is an <aperiodic Markov chain> only for $n\geq3$.

= Uniform decreasing Markov chain
{parent=Expected hitting time}
{title2=$P_{ij}=1/(i-1)\ (1\le j<i)$}

On $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, make $1$ an <absorbing state> and let each state $i>1$ move uniformly to $\{1,\ldots,i-1\}$. Its <expected hitting time> of $1$, allowing zero time when started at $1$, is $e_i=H_{i-1}$. <First-step analysis> gives $e_i=1+(i-1)^{-1}\sum_{j<i}e_j$; subtracting consecutive recurrences yields $e_i-e_{i-1}=1/(i-1)$ and hence the formula.

= Expected occupation count before absorption
{parent=Markov chain}

The expected number $u(i)$ of visits to a state $k$ before absorption satisfies the reward equation
$$
u(i)=\mathbf1_{\{i=k\}}+\sum_jP_{ij}u(j),
$$
with zero boundary data at absorbing states where counting stops.

= Killed-walk occupation voltage
{parent=Expected occupation count before absorption}
{title2=$v_{az}(u)=g_{az}(u)/\deg(u)$}

For <simple random walk> on a finite connected loopless unweighted <graph>, start at $a\ne z$ and stop on first hitting $z$. If
$$
g_{az}(u)=\mathbb E_a\sum_{t<T_z}\mathbf1_{\{X_t=u\}},\qquad v_{az}(u)=\frac{g_{az}(u)}{\deg(u)},
$$
then $v_{az}(z)=0$ and $Lv_{az}=\delta_a-\delta_z$ for the <graph Laplacian> $L$. The incoming-visit balance equation is $g_{az}(u)=\mathbf1_{\{u=a\}}+\sum_{w\sim u}g_{az}(w)/\deg(w)$ for $u\ne z$; at $z$ the Laplacian value is $-1$ because all its coordinates sum to zero. Thus $v_{az}$ is the <voltage> for unit current from $a$ to $z$, and $v_{az}(a)=R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)$. In particular, the expected number of directed transitions $u\to w$ before $T_z$ is $v_{az}(u)$.

= Four-state reflecting absorbing random walk
{parent=Expected occupation count before absorption}

For the chain with $0$ absorbing, equal-probability nearest-neighbour moves between $1,2,3$, and a probability-$1/2$ self-loop at $3$, starting from $1$ the expected absorption time is $6$, the probability of visiting $3$ before $0$ is $1/3$, and the expected number of visits to $3$ before absorption is $2$.

= Conditional hitting time
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A conditional hitting time measures time to a target under a specified successful hitting event and can be computed by weighted first-step equations.

= Periodic Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A periodic chain can return to a state only at times sharing a common divisor greater than one.

= Parity balance on entry to a period-two Markov class
{parent=Periodic Markov chain}

Suppose a <Markov chain> leaves transient states and enters a closed two-state class which alternates deterministically. If entry is always into the first state, let $a$ and $b$ be the probabilities of entering at even and odd times. After the probability of still being transient tends to zero, the even-time probabilities at the two recurrent states tend to $a,b$, while the odd-time probabilities tend to $b,a$. Individual limits therefore exist exactly when $a=b$. A periodic recurrent class can thus have convergent state probabilities from particular transient starting states even though convergence fails from its own states.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Lazy Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A lazy chain stays put with positive probability, removing periodicity without changing invariant distributions.

= Convergence to an invariant distribution
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A finite irreducible aperiodic Markov chain converges to its unique invariant distribution.

= Countable-state Markov chain convergence theorem
{parent=Convergence to an invariant distribution}
{title2=$P^n(i,j)\to\pi_j$}

For an <irreducible Markov chain> on a finite or countable state space, a <positive recurrent state> implies a unique <stationary distribution> $\pi$. If the chain is also an <aperiodic Markov chain>, its transition probabilities satisfy the displayed limit for every pair of states. The same limit holds from any initial probability distribution by bounded convergence. Positive recurrence cannot be omitted in the countably infinite case, and aperiodicity is required for ordinary, rather than averaged, convergence. This is the standard discrete-time convergence theorem; the finite-state statement alone does not cover unbounded renewal countdowns.

= Recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Markov-chain recurrence
{c}
{synonym}

= Recurrent
{synonym}

= Recurrence
{synonym}

A state is recurrent when the chain returns to it almost surely after starting there.

= Positive recurrent Markov chain
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}

= Positive recurrence
{synonym}

= Positive recurrent
{synonym}

An <irreducible Markov chain> is positive recurrent when its mean return times are finite. For a <nonexplosive Markov chain> on a countable state space, this is equivalent to the existence of a <stationary distribution> on its irreducible class. For continuous time, a return cycle includes departure and return, rather than counting immediate residence as a return.

= Positive recurrent state
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}

A recurrent state is positive recurrent when its expected return time is finite.

= Ergodic state of a Markov chain
{parent=Positive recurrent state}

In the standard discrete-time state terminology, an ergodic state is a <positive recurrent state> with <aperiodicity>. Return occurs almost surely, the mean strictly positive return time is finite, and the greatest common divisor of possible return lengths is one. An irreducible chain shares these properties among all its states.

= Null recurrent state
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}

= Null recurrent
{synonym}

A recurrent state is null recurrent when its expected return time is infinite.

= Recurrence criterion by return probabilities
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}
{wiki}

A state is recurrent exactly when the sum over time of its return probabilities diverges.

= Biased random walk hitting probability
{parent=Recurrent Markov chain}

For nearest-neighbour random walk on $\mathbb Z_+$ with upward probability $p>q$, the probability of ever hitting zero from $i$ is $(q/p)^i$.

= Random walk
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A random walk is a stochastic process formed by successive random steps.

= Upward skip-free random walk
{parent=Random walk}
{title2=$X_n\in\mathbb Z,\quad X_n\leq1$}

An upward skip-free <random walk> has integer-valued increments bounded above by one. Arbitrarily large downward jumps are permitted, but a first crossing of a nonnegative integer level $k$ from below must land exactly at $k$. This removes the overshoot from the <stopped likelihood ratio under exponential tilting> and permits the exact <geometric maximum of an upward skip-free random walk>. For a real threshold $k$, the first crossing is the crossing of $\lceil k\rceil$, so the overshoot-free formula must use that integer level.

= Geometric maximum of an upward skip-free random walk
{parent=Upward skip-free random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb P(\sup_{n\geq0}S_n=j)=(1-e^{-\lambda_0})e^{-\lambda_0j}$}

Let the increments define an <upward skip-free random walk>, and suppose $\phi(\lambda)=\mathbb E e^{\lambda X_1}$ is finite for all $\lambda\geq0$. If $\lambda_0>0$ satisfies $\phi(\lambda_0)=1$ and $\phi'(\lambda_0)>0$, the <exponential tilting> at $\lambda_0$ gives integrable increments of positive mean. The <strong law of large numbers> makes every nonnegative integer level attainable under this tilted law. Upward jumps are at most one, so the value at a first level hit is exactly that level. The <stopped likelihood ratio under exponential tilting> therefore gives hitting probability $e^{-\lambda_0 k}$ for integer $k\geq0$. Subtracting consecutive tail probabilities yields the displayed <geometric distribution> on $j=0,1,\ldots$. For real thresholds use $\lceil k\rceil$ in place of $k$.

= Gaussian random walk spends zero density of time in bounded sets
{parent=Random walk}
{title2=$n^{-1}\sum_{j\leq n}\mathbf1_B(S_j)\to0$}

For independent $N(0,1)$ increments, a <random walk> has $S_j\sim N(0,j)$. If $B$ is a bounded measurable set, its <occupation time> <probability> obeys $\mathbb P(S_j\in B)\leq |B|/\sqrt{2\pi j}$. Consequently $\mathbb E\sum_j\mathbf1_B(S_j)/j<\infty$, and <Tonelli's theorem> makes this series finite <almost surely>. <Kronecker lemma> then gives the displayed zero occupation density. This also shows why its empirical occupation <probabilities> cannot converge to a <probability> measure simultaneously on all bounded intervals and the whole real line: <continuity> from below would contradict the preserved total mass one.

= Mean-square displacement of an isotropic planar random walk
{parent=Random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb E\|S_n\|^2=n$}

Independent planar unit steps with uniformly distributed direction have zero vector <expected value>. The <squared Euclidean norm> of their sum has expectation $n$, because each diagonal term is one and all cross terms vanish by <independence>. More generally, zero-mean independent steps with common second moment $s^2$ give mean-square displacement $ns^2$; nonzero drift adds a quadratic contribution.

= Exponential martingale of a random walk
{parent=Random walk}
{title2=$e^{\theta S_n-n\kappa(\theta)}$}

For a <random walk> with <independent and identically distributed random variables> as increments and finite <moment-generating function> at $\theta$, put $\kappa(\theta)=\log\mathbb E e^{\theta X_1}$. Then $e^{\theta S_n-n\kappa(\theta)}$ is a <martingale> by conditioning on the next independent increment. In particular, if $r>0$ and $\mathbb E r^{X_1}=1$, then $r^{S_n}$ is a <martingale>. Stopping at a finite interval exit can make this process bounded, allowing the <dominated convergence theorem> to justify passage from <bounded stopping times> to the exit time.

= Stopped likelihood ratio under exponential tilting
{parent=Exponential martingale of a random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb P(T\leq n)=\mathbb E^\lambda[\phi(\lambda)^T e^{-\lambda S_T}\mathbf1_{\{T\leq n\}}]$}

For a <random walk> with <independent and identically distributed random variables> as increments, let $M_n=e^{\lambda S_n}/\phi(\lambda)^n$ define its finite-time <exponential tilting>. Under the tilted law, $L_n=M_n^{-1}$ is a <martingale>: the conditional mean of $\phi(\lambda)e^{-\lambda X_{n+1}}$ is one. For a <stopping time> $T$, partition $\{T\leq n\}$ into $\{T=j\}$ and apply the <tower property of conditional expectation> to obtain the displayed identity. Only finite-time <absolute continuity of measures> is needed; there need not be an infinite-horizon likelihood-ratio density.

= Exponential first-passage transform for an upward skip-free random walk
{parent=Exponential martingale of a random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb E[M(\tau)^{-T_b}]=e^{-\tau b}$}

Let $S_n$ have integrable, mean-zero <independent and identically distributed random variables> as increments, taking integer values at most one, with $\mathbb P(X_1=1)>0$. For a positive integer $b$, put $T_b=\inf\{n:S_n=b\}$ and $M(\tau)=\mathbb E e^{\tau X_1}$, $\tau>0$. Then $T_b$ is finite <almost surely>. Indeed, $b-S_{n\wedge T_b}$ is a nonnegative <martingale>; finite convergence on $\{T_b=\infty\}$ would force its integer increments eventually to vanish, contrary to the <Borel-Cantelli lemmas> applied to $\{X_n=1\}$.

The <exponential martingale of a random walk> $e^{\tau S_n}/M(\tau)^n$ stopped at $T_b$ is bounded by $e^{\tau b}$ because $S_{n\wedge T_b}\leq b$ and $M(\tau)\geq1$. The <dominated convergence theorem> therefore preserves its mean one at the limit and proves the displayed <first-passage time> transform. This argument accommodates arbitrarily large negative jumps; the restriction concerns upward jumps. For a <simple symmetric random walk>, $M(\tau)=\cosh\tau$, giving $\mathbb E e^{-\alpha T_b}=(e^\alpha-\sqrt{e^{2\alpha}-1})^b$.

= Exponential moment bound for a Gaussian random-walk maximum
{parent=Exponential martingale of a random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb E e^{bZ}\leq2/(2-b),\quad0<b<2$}

For <independent and identically distributed random variables> with law $N(-1,1)$, $e^{2S_n}$ is a nonnegative <martingale>, since the increment's <moment-generating function> equals one at parameter two. The <Doob maximal inequality> gives $\mathbb P(Z>t)\leq e^{-2t}$ for the all-time maximum $Z\geq0$. Integrating the tail gives the displayed bound. For $b\leq0$, $e^{bZ}\leq1$. The <strong law of large numbers> guarantees finiteness and attainment of the maximum because $S_n\to-\infty$.

= Random walk on a graph
{parent=Random walk}

A random walk on a graph moves along an incident edge at each step.

= Non-backtracking random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

A non-backtracking random walk remembers its preceding vertex and excludes that neighbor from the next choice. At vertices of degree at least two, it is a <Markov chain> on directed edges: $(a,b)$ moves to $(b,c)$ with probability $1/(\deg b-1)$ for each neighbor $c\ne a$. Dead ends require an additional convention. An initial vertex without a preceding edge uses the ordinary first-step neighbor distribution.

= Random walk on a multigraph
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

An undirected graph may have repeated edges. Choosing an incident edge uniformly gives transition probability equal to the edge multiplicity divided by the vertex degree. Its stationary probability is proportional to degree and its stationary fluxes are symmetric, making it a <reversible Markov chain>.

= Recurrent graph
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

A connected <locally finite graph> is recurrent if its <simple random walk> returns to its starting vertex <almost surely>. In that case the walk hits every fixed vertex <almost surely> from every starting vertex.

= Edge deletion preserves recurrence
{parent=Recurrent graph}

Deleting an <edge> of a locally finite <recurrent graph> leaves <recurrent Markov chain> connected components. On an infinite component choose a root away from the deleted <edge>'s endpoints, so its <vertex degree> does not change. The <Rayleigh monotonicity principle> increases resistance to wired outer boundaries, and <effective resistance to infinity> therefore remains infinite. Finite components are <recurrent Markov chain> too. This is a recurrence-type comparison; at an endpoint, changes in the <vertex degree> factor prevent a blanket monotonicity claim about first-return <probabilities>.

= Weighted graph
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{wiki}

A weighted graph assigns a symmetric nonnegative conductance $\mu_{xy}$ to each edge. With killing weights $\kappa_x$, the associated discrete-time walk moves from $x$ to $y$ with probability $\mu_{xy}/\mu_x$ and is killed with probability $\kappa_x/\mu_x$, where $\mu_x=\kappa_x+\sum_y\mu_{xy}$.

= Killing measure of a weighted graph
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Weighted graph}

The killing measure assigns the weight $\kappa_x$ of the transition from $x$ to a cemetery state.

= Electrical network
{parent=Weighted graph}
{wiki}

An electrical network is a <weighted graph> whose edge weights are interpreted as electrical conductances. Random-walk hitting quantities can then be expressed through voltages, currents, and <effective resistance>.

= Electrical current from stopped random-walk traversals
{parent=Electrical network}
{title2=$i(x,y)=w_{xy}(G(s,x)/w_x-G(s,y)/w_y)$}

In a finite connected <electrical network>, run the conductance <random walk> from $s$ until its first visit to a distinct vertex $t$. Let $G(s,x)$ be the expected visits to $x$ before stopping, and $w_x=\sum_yw_{xy}$. The expected signed number of traversals of $\{x,y\}$ is the displayed expression. Its divergence is $\mathbf1_{x=s}-\mathbf1_{x=t}$ by telescoping each path, and its resistance-weighted sum around a cycle is zero because $G(s,x)/w_x$ is a voltage. Uniqueness follows by summing $\sum_{\{x,y\}}w_{xy}(v_x-v_y)^2$: a zero-divergence voltage flow has zero energy. Thus the expected net traversals are exactly the unit source-to-sink current.

= Voltage
{title2=$u:V\to\mathbb R$}
{parent=Electrical network}

A voltage is a scalar potential on the vertices of an <electrical network>. Away from prescribed boundary vertices it is harmonic, and its difference across an edge drives current through that edge.

= Current flow
{title2=$i(x,y)$}
{parent=Electrical network}

A current flow is an antisymmetric function on oriented edges satisfying Kirchhoff's node law away from its sources and sinks. For conductance $c(x,y)$ and voltage $u$, <Ohm's law> gives $i(x,y)=c(x,y)(u(x)-u(y))$.

= Kirchhoff cycle law
{parent=Current flow}
{c}

= Kirchhoff's second law
{c}
{synonym}

= Kirchhoff's voltage law
{c}
{synonym}

The <Kirchhoff cycle law> says the directed sum of <voltage> drops around every <graph cycle> is zero. For unit <edge> resistance this is the sum of signed currents around the <graph cycle>. With unit <edge> resistance, the current is a <voltage> gradient: define the <voltage> difference by summing drops along a <graph path>. The difference of the sums along two <graph paths> is a sum around closed walks; decomposing these into <graph cycles> makes it zero. Together with the <Kirchhoff node law>, the resulting <graph Laplacian> equation determines current uniquely on a finite connected network.

= Kirchhoff node law
{parent=Current flow}
{c}

= Kirchhoff's first law
{c}
{synonym}

= Kirchhoff's current law
{c}
{synonym}

The <Kirchhoff node law> says the sum of outward currents at a <graph vertex> is zero unless that <graph vertex> is a source or sink. In an <electrical network>, it is <flow conservation>; a <unit flow> has divergences $+1$ and $-1$ at its terminals.

= Effective resistance
{title2=$R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)$}
{parent=Electrical network}
{wiki}

The effective resistance between vertices $a$ and $z$ is the voltage difference required to send one unit of current from $a$ to $z$.

= Return probability from effective resistance
{parent=Effective resistance}
{title2=$P_x(T_x^+<T_y)=1-(d(x)R(x,y))^{-1}$}

For the conductance-weighted <random walk on a graph>, set $d(x)=\sum_vc_{xv}$. The voltage $h(u)=P_u(T_x<T_y)$ has boundary values one and zero. The total <electric current> is $\sum_vc_{xv}(1-h(v))=1/R(x,y)$. The <first-step analysis> therefore gives the displayed positive-return-time formula. Wiring the exterior of finite sets and taking limits gives $P_x(T_x^+=\infty)=1/(d(x)R(x,\infty))$.

= Foster's theorem
{parent=Effective resistance}
{c}
{title2=$\sum_e c_eR_{\mathrm{eff}}(e)=|V|-1$}

= Foster theorem
{synonym}
{c}

For a finite connected unweighted loopless <graph> with $n$ <graph vertices>,
$$
\sum_{e\in E}R_{\mathrm{eff}}(e)=n-1.
$$
The <edge-inclusion formula for a uniform spanning tree> gives the left-hand side as $\mathbb E|T|$, and every <spanning tree> has $n-1$ <edges>. On the same finite <connected graph> with positive <edge> conductances $c_e>0$, the corresponding identity is $\sum_e c_eR_{\mathrm{eff}}(e)=n-1$.

= Effective resistance to infinity
{parent=Effective resistance}
{title2=$R_{\mathrm{eff}}(o\leftrightarrow\infty)$}

= Effective resistances to infinity
{synonym}

The effective resistance to infinity is the limit of the <effective resistance> from a fixed vertex to the wired complement of increasing finite sets. It is finite exactly when the <finite-energy flow criterion for transience> holds.

= Rayleigh monotonicity principle
{c}
{parent=Effective resistance}

Increasing edge resistances, including deleting edges, cannot decrease <effective resistance>. Decreasing resistances or identifying vertices cannot increase <effective resistance>. These statements also follow from the <Thomson principle>.

= Effective conductance
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{eff}}=1/R_{\mathrm{eff}}$}
{parent=Effective resistance}

The effective conductance between two terminals is the reciprocal of their <effective resistance>. Under a unit voltage difference it equals the total current flowing from one terminal to the other.

= Thomson principle
{c}
{parent=Effective resistance}
{wiki}

<Thomson principle> says that effective resistance is the minimum energy among unit flows from $a$ to $z$:
$$
R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)=\inf_\theta\sum_e\frac{\theta(e)^2}{c(e)}.
$$

= Nash-Williams inequality
{c}
{parent=Effective resistance}
{wiki}

If $\Pi_1,\ldots,\Pi_m$ are pairwise edge-disjoint cutsets separating $a$ from $z$ in an <electrical network>, then
$$
R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)\geq\sum_{k=1}^m\left(\sum_{e\in\Pi_k}c(e)\right)^{-1}.
$$

= Commute time identity
{parent=Effective resistance}

For a random walk on a finite <electrical network>,
$$
\mathbb E_aT_z+\mathbb E_zT_a
=2\left(\sum_{e\in E}c(e)\right)R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z).
$$

= Directed edge occupation in a random-walk commute
{parent=Commute time identity}
{title2=$\mathbb E S(u,v)=R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)$}

Let <simple random walk> travel from $a\ne z$ to its first visit to $z$, then stop on its subsequent first hit of $a$ in a finite connected unweighted loopless <graph>. Every directed <edge> $u\to v$ has expected traversal count
$$
\mathbb E S(u,v)=R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z).
$$
The <strong Markov property> splits the count into the two killed legs. By <killed-walk occupation voltage>, their contributions are $v_{az}(u)$ and $v_{za}(u)$. Their Laplacians cancel, so the <harmonic maximum principle on a finite graph> makes the sum constant, with value $v_{az}(a)+v_{za}(a)=R_{\mathrm{eff}}(a,z)$. Summing over all directed <edges> recovers the <commute time identity>.

= Bishop random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

A bishop random walk moves uniformly among the current square and all chessboard squares sharing a diagonal with it.

= Transient graph
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

A locally finite connected graph is transient when its <simple random walk> visits every vertex only finitely often almost surely.

= Green function of a transient weighted graph
{title2=$g(x,y)$}
{parent=Transient graph}

For the walk on a <weighted graph>, the Green function normalized by vertex weight is
$$
g(x,y)=\frac1{\mu_y}\mathbb E_x\sum_{n\geq0}\mathbf1_{\{X_n=y\}}.
$$
Symmetry of the conductances makes $g(x,y)=g(y,x)$, and $\mathcal E(g(\mathord\cdot,y),f)=f(y)$ for finitely supported $f$.

= Green-function decay for simple random walk on the integer lattice
{parent=Green function of a transient weighted graph}

For <simple random walk> on $\mathbb Z^d$ with $d\geq3$,
$$
g(0,x)\asymp |x|^{2-d}.
$$
Consequently the probability of ever hitting $x$ from the origin has the same order.

= Equilibrium potential of a finite set
{title2=$h_A$}
{parent=Transient graph}

For a finite vertex set $A$, its equilibrium potential is $h_A(x)=P_x(H_A<\infty)$. It equals one on $A$, is harmonic off $A$, and belongs to the <Dirichlet energy space with zero boundary at infinity>.

= Equilibrium measure of a finite set
{title2=$e_A$}
{parent=Equilibrium potential of a finite set}

The equilibrium measure is supported on $A$ and is given by
$$
e_A(x)=\mathbf1_{\{x\in A\}}\mu_xP_x(\widetilde H_A=\infty).
$$

= Capacity of a finite set for a transient random walk
{title2=$\operatorname{cap}(A)$}
{parent=Equilibrium measure of a finite set}

The capacity of a finite set is the total mass of its <equilibrium measure of a finite set>:
$$
\operatorname{cap}(A)=\sum_xe_A(x)=\mathcal E(h_A,h_A).
$$
Capacity is monotone under inclusion, though strict inclusion need not give strict inequality.

= Singleton equilibrium potential from the Green function
{parent=Equilibrium potential of a finite set}

For a vertex $o$,
$$
P_x(H_o<\infty)=\frac{g(x,o)}{g(o,o)},
\qquad
\mathcal E(h_{\{o\}},h_{\{o\}})=\frac1{g(o,o)}.
$$

= Last-exit decomposition for a transient random walk
{parent=Transient graph}

A last-exit decomposition partitions a transient path event according to the final visit to a finite set. Reversing the finite path before that visit and using <detailed balance> converts last-exit probabilities into hitting probabilities weighted by an <equilibrium measure of a finite set>.

= Loop-erased random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{wiki}

A loop-erased random walk is the self-avoiding path obtained by chronologically erasing loops from a random-walk path.

= Loop-erasure of planar Brownian motion
{parent=Loop-erased random walk}

The time reversal of a <Brownian motion conditioned to exit at a boundary point> can be coupled to radial $\operatorname{SLE}_2$ so that its loop-erasure is the radial curve. The coupling requires the Brownian path's first hit of each initial radial segment to occur at its tip. It is an existence theorem for a coupling and does not assert that the Brownian trace equals the simple radial curve, or that an elementary deterministic finite-loop algorithm is defined for all continuum paths.

= Uniform-spanning-tree path representation of loop-erased random walk
{parent=Loop-erased random walk}

On a finite connected unweighted <graph>, the <graph path> between $x$ and $y$ in a <uniform spanning tree> has the law of <loop-erased random walk> from $x$ to $y$. Root <Wilson's algorithm> at $y$ and process $x$ first: the first attached <graph path> is exactly that <loop erasure>, and later attachments cannot change it. Changing the root to $x$ proves <reversibility of loop-erased random walk>, even though chronological <loop erasure> does not commute with reversing each individual random-walk trajectory.

= Loop erasure
{parent=Loop-erased random walk}

Chronological loop erasure removes each loop from a finite or transient infinite path as soon as the loop is formed, producing a self-avoiding path.

= Reversibility of loop-erased random walk
{parent=Loop-erased random walk}

On a finite undirected graph, the reversal of a loop-erased random walk from $x$ to $y$ has the law of a loop-erased random walk from $y$ to $x$. The uniform-spanning-tree path representation proves the identity.

= Simple random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

A simple random walk on a locally finite graph chooses each neighbouring vertex with equal probability.

= Simple random walk on the hypercube
{parent=Simple random walk}

On the $n$-dimensional <hypercube graph>, simple random walk flips one uniformly chosen coordinate at each step. Its stationary distribution is uniform on the $2^n$ vertices, so its mean return time is $2^n$.

= Spectrum of lazy random walk on the hypercube
{parent=Simple random walk on the hypercube}
{title2=$\lambda_k=1-k/n$}

For <random walk> on $\{-1,1\}^n$ that chooses a coordinate uniformly and flips it with probability $1/2$, the <Walsh characters> $f_J(x)=\prod_{j\in J}x_j$ form an <orthonormal eigenbasis> under the <uniform distribution on a finite set>. Flipping a coordinate in $J$ negates $f_J$, so $Pf_J=(1-|J|/n)f_J$. Hence $1-k/n$ has <multiplicity> $\binom nk$, and the ordinary and absolute <spectral gaps> both equal $1/n$.

= Simple random walk in two dimensions is recurrent
{parent=Simple random walk}

A simple random walk on $\mathbb Z^2$ visits its starting point infinitely often almost surely and hits every fixed vertex almost surely.

= Simple random walk on the integer line
{parent=Random walk on a graph}

If the independent increments are $+1$ with probability $p$ and $-1$ with probability $q=1-p$, then
$$
S_n=2B_n-n,
\qquad B_n\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p).
$$
Thus $\mathbb ES_n=n(p-q)$ and $\operatorname{var}(S_n)=4npq$.

= Expected visits to the origin by a simple random walk
{parent=Simple random walk on the integer line}

For the symmetric walk and $V_{2n}=|\{0\leq j\leq2n:S_j=0\}|$,
$$
\mathbb EV_{2n}=\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{\binom{2k}{k}}{4^k}.
$$
Since the central binomial coefficient satisfies $\binom{2k}{k}/4^k\asymp k^{-1/2}$, this expectation is of order $\sqrt n$.

= Simultaneous returns of independent simple random walks
{parent=Simple random walk on the integer line}

For $d$ <independent random variables>[independent] symmetric walks on $\mathbb Z$, the probability that all are at the origin at time $2n$ is
$$
\left(\frac{\binom{2n}{n}}{4^n}\right)^d\asymp n^{-d/2}.
$$
The <Borel-Cantelli lemmas> therefore show that simultaneous returns occur only finitely often almost surely when $d\geq3$.

= Stationary distribution of a graph random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{wiki}

On a finite undirected graph, stationary mass is proportional to vertex degree.

= Biased random walk
{parent=Random walk on a graph}
{wiki}

A biased nearest-neighbor walk steps right and left with unequal probabilities.

= Hitting time of a downward-biased walk reflected at an upper barrier
{parent=Biased random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb E_NT_0=\frac N{q-p}-\frac{2pq}{(q-p)^2}\left(1-(p/q)^N\right)$}

On $\{0,\ldots,N\}$, stop at zero, move left with probability $q>1/2$ and right with probability $p=1-q$ at interior states, and move deterministically from $N$ to $N-1$. For $m_i=\mathbb E_iT_0$, the <first-step analysis> gives $m_0=0$, $m_i=1+qm_{i-1}+pm_{i+1}$ and $m_N=1+m_{N-1}$. Setting $D_i=m_i-m_{i-1}$ gives $qD_i-pD_{i+1}=1$, $D_N=1$, hence $D_i=[1-2p(p/q)^{N-i}]/(q-p)$. Summing these differences proves the displayed formula, and more generally $m_i=i/(q-p)-2pq(p/q)^{N-i}[1-(p/q)^i]/(q-p)^2$. A downward block has a uniformly positive probability of reaching zero, which supplies a geometric tail bound and justifies finiteness of these expectations.

= Diffusion limit of a weakly biased random walk
{parent=Biased random walk}
{title2=$V=a(\alpha-\beta)/\tau,\ D=a^2/(2\tau)$}

For independent lattice steps of length $a$ at intervals $\tau$, with probabilities $\alpha+\beta=1$, the diffusive scaling $a^2/\tau\to2D$ and $(\alpha-\beta)a/\tau\to V$ requires a bias of order $a$. Expanding the <master equation> gives the <advection-diffusion equation> $p_t+Vp_x=Dp_{xx}$. The finite-step variance coefficient is $2a^2\alpha\beta/\tau$; it has the same limit because the squared bias vanishes. Fixed nonzero bias does not give finite drift under this diffusive scaling.

= Maximum of a downward-biased random walk
{parent=Biased random walk}
{title2=$\mathbb P(M=j)=(1-p/q)(p/q)^j,\quad j\geq0$}

For a nearest-neighbour <random walk> with upward probability $0<p<q=1-p$, start at $S_0=0$ and put $M=\sup_{n\geq0}S_n$. Its <probability distribution> is geometric on the nonnegative integers, with the displayed mass and <expected value> $p/(q-p)$. To prove this, the <exponential martingale of a biased simple random walk> $Z_n=(q/p)^{S_n}$ has expectation one. Stop at the first hit $H_k$ of $k$. Before that hit, $Z_{n\wedge H_k}\leq(q/p)^k$; the <strong law of large numbers> gives $S_n\to-\infty$ on paths without a hit. <Dominated convergence theorem> therefore gives $1=(q/p)^k\mathbb P(H_k<\infty)$. Taking differences of $\mathbb P(M\geq k)=(p/q)^k$ yields the mass. If time zero is excluded, the maximum can equal $-1$ with probability $q-p$ and does not have this nonnegative <geometric distribution>.

= Reflected biased random walk on the nonnegative integers
{parent=Biased random walk}

Let a discrete-time chain move up with probability $q$ and down with probability $p=1-q$ away from zero, while at zero it moves up with probability $q$ and stays with probability $p$. It is transient for $q>p$, null recurrent for $q=p$, and positive recurrent for $q<p$. In the positive-recurrent case its invariant distribution is
$$
\pi_k=(1-r)r^k,
\qquad r=\frac qp,
$$
and aperiodicity from the self-loop at zero implies convergence to this distribution.

= Gambler's ruin
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

Gambler's ruin studies a nearest-neighbour random walk stopped on reaching either endpoint of a finite interval.

= Duration of gambler's ruin conditioned on a chosen boundary
{parent=Gambler's ruin}
{title2=$\mathbb E_i[\tau_0\mid\tau_0<\tau_r]=\frac{i(2r-i)}3$}

For a simple symmetric random walk absorbed at $0,r$, the probability of absorption at $0$ is $h_i=(r-i)/r$. Conditioning on that absorption gives the <Doob h-transform> with transition probabilities $(r-i+1)/[2(r-i)]$ downwards and $(r-i-1)/[2(r-i)]$ upwards. If $g_i=\mathbb E_i[\tau\mathbf1_{\{\tau_0<\tau_r\}}]$, first-step analysis gives $g_i=h_i+(g_{i-1}+g_{i+1})/2$, $g_0=g_r=0$. Its solution is $g_i=i(r-i)(2r-i)/(3r)$, and dividing by $h_i$ gives the conditional duration.

= Discounted gambler's ruin hitting probability
{parent=Gambler's ruin}
{title2=$u_k=\mathbb E_k[s^\tau\mathbf1_{\{X_\tau=0\}}]$}

For a nearest-neighbour <random walk> on $\{0,\ldots,N\}$ with right-step probability $p$, left-step probability $q=1-p$, and absorption time $\tau$, first-step conditioning gives $u_k=s(pu_{k+1}+qu_{k-1})$, $u_0=1$, $u_N=0$. For $0<s<1$, the two positive distinct characteristic roots are $r_\pm=(1\pm\sqrt{1-4pqs^2})/(2ps)$. Solving the boundary equations gives $u_k=(r_+^Nr_-^k-r_-^Nr_+^k)/(r_+^N-r_-^N)$. The maximum principle $\|u-v\|_\infty\leq s\|u-v\|_\infty$ proves uniqueness for equal boundary data. This is the joint generating function of the absorption time and the lower-end absorption event.

= Expected duration of biased gambler's ruin
{parent=Gambler's ruin}
{title2=$m_i=[N(1-\rho^i)/(1-\rho^N)-i]/(p-q)$}

For a nearest-neighbour fortune on $\{0,\ldots,N\}$, with win probability $p$, loss probability $q=1-p$ and $p\ne q$, put $\rho=q/p$. <First-step analysis> gives $m_i=1+pm_{i+1}+qm_{i-1}$, with zero endpoint values. Solving the recurrence gives the displayed mean duration. Its symmetric limit is $i(N-i)$.

= Biased gambler's ruin probability
{parent=Gambler's ruin}

For a walk that steps right with probability $p>1/2$, put $\lambda=(1-p)/p$. If $a<0<b$ and $T_x$ is the first hitting time of $x$, then the martingale $\lambda^{S_n}$ and the <optional stopping theorem> give
$$
\mathbb P(T_a<T_b)
=\frac{\lambda^b-1}{\lambda^b-\lambda^a}.
$$
Letting the opposite boundary tend to infinity gives
$$
\mathbb P(T_a<\infty)=\lambda^{-a},
\qquad
\mathbb P(T_b<\infty)=1.
$$

= Expected duration of symmetric gambler's ruin
{parent=Gambler's ruin}

For a symmetric random walk started at $i\in\{-a,\ldots,b\}$ and stopped on first reaching $-a$ or $b$, the mean duration is
$$
\mathbb E_iT=(i+a)(b-i).
$$
In particular, $\mathbb E_0T=ab$. This follows either from the first-step recurrence or by stopping the martingale $S_n^2-n$.

= Strong Markov property
{parent=Markov chain}
{c}
{wiki}

At an almost surely finite stopping time, a Markov process restarts from its stopped state with the same transition law and independently of the history conditional on that state.

= Maximum of a killed telegraph integral
{parent=Strong Markov property}
{title2=$\mathbb P(\sup_{t\geq0}\int_0^tX_sds>c\mid X_0=1)=e^{-c\sqrt{\lambda(2q+\lambda)}}$}

Let $X$ switch between $\pm1$ at rate $q$ and be killed into zero at rate $\lambda$. To exceed a new level it must cross it in state $+1$. Restarting there by the <strong Markov property> gives the multiplicative identity $\psi_+(c+d)=\psi_+(c)\psi_+(d)$, hence an exponential tail. First-step conditioning gives $\psi_-(c)=\int_0^\infty qe^{-(q+\lambda)u}\psi_+(c+u)du$ and $\psi_+(c)=e^{-(q+\lambda)c}+\int_0^cqe^{-(q+\lambda)u}\psi_-(c-u)du$. Substituting the exponential determines its rate as $\sqrt{\lambda(2q+\lambda)}$.

= Two-state excursion visit law
{parent=Strong Markov property}

Let $a=\mathbb P_i(T_j<T_i)$ and $b=\mathbb P_j(T_i<T_j)$. Before returning to $i$, the chain visits $j$ zero times with probability $1-a$ and, for $k\geq1$, exactly $k$ times with probability
$$
ab(1-b)^{k-1}.
$$
When $b>0$, the expected number of visits is $a/b$.

= Stationary cycle occupation formula
{parent=Markov chain}

For an irreducible positive-recurrent Markov chain with invariant distribution $\pi$, the expected number of visits to $j$ during one return cycle from $i$ to $i$ is
$$
\frac{\pi(j)}{\pi(i)}.
$$
Combining this with the two-state excursion visit law gives
$$
\frac{\mathbb P_i(T_j<T_i)}{\mathbb P_j(T_i<T_j)}
=\frac{\pi(j)}{\pi(i)}.
$$

= Markov property
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Markov
{c}
{synonym}

The Markov property says that, conditional on the present state, the future evolution is independent of the past.

= Transition operator
{parent=Markov property}
{title2=$(Ph)(s)=\mathbb E[h(S_{t+1})\mid S_t=s]$}

The operator that averages a function over the next-state conditional law of a time-homogeneous <Markov process>. For a <random walk> with increments of law $\nu$ independent of the past including the initial state, $(Ph)(s)=\int h(s+y)\nu(dy)$. This additive operator preserves <convexity> when the integrals exist.

= Markov factorization
{c}
{parent=Markov property}

For an ordered Markov sequence, the joint density factors into an initial density and one-step transition densities:
$$
p(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=p(x_1)\prod_{i=2}^np(x_i\mid x_{i-1}).
$$

= Time-homogeneous Markov property
{parent=Markov property}

The time-homogeneous Markov property says that the conditional law of a future state depends only on the current state and the elapsed time, not on the current calendar time or earlier history.

= Continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

A continuous-time Markov chain waits an exponential time of rate $q_i$ in state $i$ and then jumps according to probabilities $p_{ij}=q_{ij}/q_i$.

= Nonexplosion of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\#\{\text{jumps in }[0,t]\}<\infty\text{ almost surely}$}

= Nonexplosion
{synonym}

A <continuous-time Markov chain> is nonexplosive if it makes only finitely many jumps on every bounded time interval. A uniformly bounded total jump intensity permits domination by a <Poisson process>. More generally, if a population process stays in a finite random set of states on each finite interval and every individual state has finite exit rate, localizing to fixed finite sets gives the same conclusion. In an <open migration process>, external Poisson immigration bounds the total population while internal moves preserve it and exits reduce it, giving this localization.

= Markov jump process
{c}
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

A Markov jump process is a nonexplosive, piecewise-constant continuous-time Markov process. Transition rates $q(x,y)$ give exponential holding time of rate $\sum_{y\ne x}q(x,y)$ and next-state probabilities proportional to these rates. Being Markov relative to a specified filtration requires the conditional future law given that filtration to depend only on the present state.

// Target: probability-and-statistics.bigb

= Symmetric stochastic attrition process
{parent=Markov jump process}
{title2=$(a,b)\to(a-1,b)\text{ at rate }\kappa b,\quad(a,b)\to(a,b-1)\text{ at rate }\kappa a$}

This finite-state <Markov jump process> models two opposing populations, each suffering losses at a rate proportional to the surviving opposing population. Both coordinate axes are absorbing. Its embedded <jump chain> removes one individual at each step, with probabilities $b/(a+b)$ and $a/(a+b)$. The corresponding deterministic equations $\dot a=-\kappa b$, $\dot b=-\kappa a$ conserve $a^2-b^2$; finite stochastic populations satisfy a near-invariant instead.

= Squared-population near-invariant for stochastic attrition
{parent=Symmetric stochastic attrition process}
{title2=$D_j=A_j^2-B_j^2,\quad\mathbb E(\Delta D_j\mid\mathcal F_{j-1})=\frac{B_{j-1}-A_{j-1}}{A_{j-1}+B_{j-1}}$}

In the <jump chain> of the <symmetric stochastic attrition process>, the two possible increments of $D$ are $-2A+1$ and $2B-1$. Their mean is the displayed quantity, bounded by one in absolute value. Subtracting these conditional means produces a <martingale>. If both initial populations are at most $N$, it has at most $2N$ nonzero increments, each bounded by $4N$. Thus <Azuma-Hoeffding inequality> controls changes of order $N^2$ with exponentially small probability of order $e^{-cN}$.

= Exponential concentration of terminal stochastic attrition survivors
{parent=Squared-population near-invariant for stochastic attrition}
{title2=$\mathbb P(|X^N-\sqrt{1-\alpha^2}|>\delta)\leq2e^{-(1-\alpha^2)\delta^2N/256}$}

Start the <symmetric stochastic attrition process> at $(N,\lfloor\alpha N\rfloor)$, with $0<\alpha<1$, and let $X^N$ be the final first-population size divided by $N$. The bound holds for all sufficiently large $N$, depending on $\alpha,\delta>0$. At absorption, one coordinate is zero. If the first survives, the terminal squared difference is $N^2(X^N)^2$; if it is extinguished, that difference is nonpositive. In either case the indicated deviation forces an error of at least $\sqrt{1-\alpha^2}\,\delta N^2$ from the limiting squared invariant. The initial rounding error and total conditional drift are only $O(N)$. Apply <Azuma-Hoeffding inequality> to the centered <squared-population near-invariant for stochastic attrition>, using its $2N$ increments bounded by $4N$, to obtain the displayed exponent.

= Single marked exponential jump process
{parent=Markov jump process}
{title2=$X_t=U\mathbf1_{\{T\leq t\}},\quad T\sim\operatorname{Exp}(1)$}

Let $T$ be a rate-one <exponential random variable> independent of a <uniform random variable> $U$ on $(0,1)$. In its natural <filtration>, this <Markov jump process> jumps once from zero to its mark and then stays there. Its destination rate kernel is $q(x,dy)=\mathbf1_{\{x=0\}}\mathbf1_{(0,1)}(y)dy$. For a Borel mark set $B$, the <martingale> $\mathbf1_{\{T\leq t,U\in B\}}-|B|(t\wedge T)$ follows directly from the memoryless residual lifetime. Information about the future mark or lifetime must not be inserted into the initial filtration.

= Lévy kernel of a Markov jump process
{parent=Markov jump process}
{c}
{title2=$K(x,dz)$}

A kernel specifying the jump rate from state $x$ for each displacement $z$. With the displacement convention the <infinitesimal generator> is $L\varphi(x)=\int(\varphi(x+z)-\varphi(x))K(x,dz)$. The destination convention instead records a rate measure on the new state, translating each displacement by $x$. For finite total rate, that total is the exponential holding rate and the normalized measure is the next-jump distribution.

= Jump measure of a Markov jump process
{parent=Markov jump process}
{title2=$\mu=\sum_{t:\,X_t\ne X_{t-}}\delta_{(t,X_t)},\quad\nu(dt,dy)=q(X_{t-},dy)dt$}

For a <Markov jump process> with off-diagonal rate kernel $q(x,dy)$, the jump measure records the jump time and the destination state. Relative to a filtration for which the process has the Markov jump property, its predictable compensator is the displayed rate measure, up to explosion. The corresponding generator is $Qf(x)=\int[f(y)-f(x)]q(x,dy)$. An enlarged filtration revealing future jumps need not preserve this compensator, so the filtration condition is essential.

= Compensated jump-measure local martingale
{parent=Jump measure of a Markov jump process}
{title2=$H*(\mu-\nu)$}

A predictable mark-dependent integrand $H(t,y)$ gives a true <martingale> $\int_0^t\int H(s,y)(\mu-\nu)(ds,dy)$ whenever $\mathbb E\int_0^t\int|H|d\nu<\infty$ on every finite horizon. Local versions follow when this expected integrability holds after localization. The compensator identity applied to predictable indicators from the past proves that every increment has conditional expectation zero. Choosing $H(s,y)=f(y)-f(X_{s-})$ gives the generator martingale $f(X_t)-f(X_0)-\int_0^tQf(X_s)ds$ under the same local integrability conditions.

= Density-dependent Markov jump process
{parent=Markov jump process}

With scale $N$, such a process has finitely many possible jump vectors $\ell/N$ occurring at rates $N\beta_\ell(x)$ depending on the current scaled state. Its drift is $\sum_\ell\ell\beta_\ell(x)$, while the predictable covariance rate of its martingale fluctuations is $N^{-1}\sum_\ell\ell\ell^T\beta_\ell(x)$.

// Target: probability-and-statistics.bigb

= Martingale decomposition of a density-dependent jump process
{parent=Density-dependent Markov jump process}
{title2=$Z_t^N=Z_0^N+\int_0^tF(Z_s^N)ds+M_t^N$}

With jumps $\ell/N$ at rates $N\beta_\ell(z)$, compensate each jump count by its integrated rate. The drift is $F(z)=\sum_\ell\ell\beta_\ell(z)$ and the <predictable quadratic variation> matrix of the <martingale> term is $N^{-1}\int_0^t\sum_\ell\ell\ell^{\mathsf T}\beta_\ell(Z_s^N)ds$. On a compact region with bounded rates this bracket is of order $1/N$. A Lipschitz drift and the <Gronwall inequality> transfer martingale concentration to the <fluid limit>.

= Destination-dependent reset chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$Q=\mathbf1r^T-RI$}

For jump rates $q_{ij}=r_j$ when $i\ne j$, let $R=\sum_jr_j>0$. The positive-rate states form the unique closed communicating class; every zero-rate state is an open singleton. The stationary law is $\pi_i=r_i/R$, with detailed balance $\pi_iq_{ij}=r_ir_j/R$. When at least two rates are positive, the embedded jump-chain law is proportional to $r_i(R-r_i)$. With only one positive rate, that state is absorbing and the genuine sequence of jump times stops there; an extended discrete chain can use an absorbing self-loop.

= Hitting probabilities for a velocity-switching Markov ladder
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\vartheta=\mu(\lambda+\alpha)/[\lambda(\mu+\beta)]$}

On nonnegative levels, a chain moves right at rate $\lambda$ in state $C$, left at rate $\mu$ in state $W$, and switches $C\to W$ at rate $\alpha$ and $W\to C$ at rate $\beta$. The minimal nonnegative solution for hitting $W_0$ is identically one when $\mu\alpha\ge\lambda\beta$. When $\mu\alpha<\lambda\beta$, it is $h_{W_i}=\vartheta^i$ and $h_{C_i}=b\vartheta^i$, where $b=\alpha(\mu+\beta)/[\beta(\lambda+\alpha)]<1$. These cases give recurrence and transience respectively.

= Nonexplosive continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

A continuous-time <Markov chain> with finite holding rates is nonexplosive when its jump times have no finite accumulation almost surely. It then remains in its specified state space at every finite time, rather than being lost to a cemetery state after infinitely many jumps. Bounded rates are sufficient for nonexplosion, but finite rates at individual states alone are not.

= Absorption-or-escape dichotomy for a chain with killing
{parent=Nonexplosive continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\mathbb P(\text{absorption or }X_t\to\infty)=1$}

For a <nonexplosive continuous-time Markov chain> on the nonnegative integers, suppose zero is absorbing and each positive state has positive rate to zero. On every finite positive set the conditional chance of absorption at the next jump has a positive minimum. By the <strong Markov property>, the chance of surviving $r$ departures from that set is bounded by $(1-\delta)^r$. Without absorption only finitely many such departures occur, and finite positive holding rates prohibit staying forever at a positive state. Thus eventually the path stays outside each finite set: it escapes to infinity. This conclusion needs no upward skip-free assumption.

= Equilibrium distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\pi P(t)=\pi$}

An equilibrium probability row vector is invariant under every time transition matrix, and satisfies $\pi Q=0$ when generator sums are defined. A finite irreducible chain has a unique such distribution and all transition rows converge to it. With absorbing states, every probability mixture on the closed absorbing states is an equilibrium, and limiting rows are determined by absorption probabilities. For a jump chain with stationary weights $\alpha_i$ and positive holding rates $\lambda_i$, the corresponding time-stationary weights are proportional to $\alpha_i/\lambda_i$.

= Transition intensity matrix
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$Q=(q_{rs})$}

= Infinitesimal generator matrix
{synonym}

For a finite-state <continuous-time Markov chain>, a transition intensity matrix has nonnegative off-diagonal rates $q_{rs}$ and $q_{rr}=-\sum_{s\ne r}q_{rs}$. Every row sums to zero. In the time-homogeneous case its <transition probability matrix> is the <matrix exponential> $P(t)=e^{tQ}$. An <absorbing state> has a zero row.

= Ergodic theorem for a finite continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$T^{-1}\int_0^Tf(X_t)\,dt\to\sum_n\pi(n)f(n)$}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> in continuous time, time averages converge almost surely to averages under its unique <stationary distribution>, from any initial state. Applying this to an activation indicator yields the corresponding <throughput>.

= Nonexplosive Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

A <continuous-time Markov chain> is nonexplosive when it makes only finitely many jumps in every finite time interval, almost surely. Uniformly bounded total jump rates suffice. Another useful criterion bounds the states visited over each finite interval by a random finite set on which rates are bounded.

= Global balance for a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\sum_{m\ne n}\pi(m)q(m,n)=\pi(n)\sum_{m\ne n}q(n,m)$}

= Stationary balance equations
{synonym}

Global balance equates total stationary incoming and outgoing <transition intensities> at each state. <Detailed balance for a continuous-time Markov chain> is sufficient but stronger: it equates each pair separately.

= Poisson time-change representation of a Markov chain
{c}
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$X(t)=X(0)+\sum_k\nu_kP_k(\int_0^t a_k(X(s-))\,ds)$}

= Random time-change representation
{synonym}

A jump process with event types $k$, increments $\nu_k$, and rates $a_k(X)$ can be represented as $X(t)=X(0)+\sum_k\nu_k P_k(\int_0^t a_k(X(s-))\,ds)$, with independent unit-rate <Poisson processes>. This represents the <Markov jump-process generator> through time-changed counts.

= Interchange process
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

Place one distinct label at each vertex of a finite connected weighted <graph>. At rate $c_{uv}=c_{vu}\geq0$, interchange the labels at $u$ and $v$. This <continuous-time Markov chain> has a <uniform distribution on a finite set> as its <stationary distribution>. The position of one label is the <random walk> with the same rates.

= Aldous spectral gap theorem
{c}
{parent=Interchange process}

On a finite connected weighted <graph>, the <interchange process> and the single-label <random walk> with the same symmetric rates have equal <spectral gaps>. This is a substantial theorem, not just the elementary inclusion of the single-label <spectrum> in the full <spectrum>. A proof is given in https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.1238[Proof of Aldous' spectral gap conjecture], Theorem 1.1.

= Time reversal of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$q^*_{ij}=\pi_jq_{ji}/\pi_i$}

For an equilibrium chain with strictly positive stationary probabilities, reversed jump rates are $q^*_{ij}=\pi_jq_{ji}/\pi_i$. Under <detailed balance> they equal the original rates, making the stationary path law reversible. Holding times and jump directions reverse together; this is more than symmetry of one-time distributions.

= Kingman's coalescent
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{c}

A partition-valued <Markov chain> in which every unordered pair of current blocks merges at rate one. On $n$ initial singleton blocks, the holding time with $j$ blocks is exponential with rate $\binom j2$. Restrictions to subsets of labels have the same law.

= Mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree
{parent=Kingman's coalescent}
{title2=$S=\sum_{j=2}^nY_j$}

In <Kingman's coalescent>, the epoch with $j$ ancestral lineages lasts $T_j\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\binom j2)$, independently across epochs. If each lineage carries an independent <Poisson process> of <mutations> with rate $\theta/2$, its epoch mutation count satisfies $Y_j\mid T_j\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(j\theta T_j/2)$. The total mutation count is $S=\sum_{j=2}^nY_j$. Under the <infinite sites mutation model>, this also counts <segregating sites>; without that assumption it still counts mutation events, which need not correspond one-to-one to observed polymorphisms.

= Moments of the mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree
{parent=Mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree}
{title2=$\mathbb ES=\theta H_{n-1},\quad\operatorname{Var}S=\theta H_{n-1}+\theta^2H_{n-1}^{(2)}$}

The <independent mutation counts in coalescent epochs> have the <geometric mutation count in a coalescent epoch> laws. Summing their <expectations> and <variances> gives the displayed formulas, where $H_m=\sum_{i=1}^m1/i$ and $H_m^{(2)}=\sum_{i=1}^m1/i^2$. The extra quadratic term records randomness of the ancestral tree, beyond the conditional <Poisson distribution> variance.

= Geometric mutation count in a coalescent epoch
{parent=Mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree}
{title2=$Y_j\sim\operatorname{Geom}_0\left(\frac{j-1}{j-1+\theta}\right)$}

Integrating the <Poisson distribution> <probability generating function> over the <exponential distribution> of the epoch length gives
$$
\mathbb E[s^{Y_j}]=\frac{j-1}{j-1+\theta(1-s)}.
$$
Thus $Y_j$ has a <geometric distribution> on $\{0,1,\ldots\}$, with <expectation> $\theta/(j-1)$ and <variance> $\theta/(j-1)+\theta^2/(j-1)^2$. The support convention matters: the count includes zero mutations.

= Independent mutation counts in coalescent epochs
{parent=Mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree}

The epoch lengths in <Kingman's coalescent> are <independent random variables>. Conditional on these lengths, <mutation> counts on disjoint tree pieces are independent <Poisson random variables>. Their conditional laws depend only on the corresponding epoch length, so integrating the product of conditional laws against the independent epoch laws leaves independent unconditional epoch mutation counts. This justifies adding their <variances>, rather than assuming independence from conditional independence alone.

= Total branch length of a neutral coalescent
{parent=Kingman's coalescent}
{title2=$L=\sum_{j=2}^n jT_j$}

In coalescent units, independent waiting times satisfy $T_j\sim\operatorname{Exp}(j(j-1)/2)$, and total branch length is $L=\sum_{j=2}^n jT_j$. Therefore $EL=2\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1/i$. The variables $jT_j/2$ are independent exponentials with rates $j-1$, so $W=L/2$ has the same distribution as the maximum of $n-1$ independent unit exponentials, with density $(n-1)e^{-w}(1-e^{-w})^{n-2}$. The last identity follows either from its Laplace transform or from the independent spacings of exponential order statistics.

= Kingman's infinite coalescent
{parent=Kingman's coalescent}
{c}
{title2=$\Pi_t$}

The consistent partition process on $\mathbb N$ whose finite restrictions are <Kingman's coalescent>. It starts with infinitely many singleton blocks.

= Coming down from infinity of the Kingman coalescent
{parent=Kingman's infinite coalescent}

For $t>0$, the number $B_t$ of blocks is finite almost surely. On a finite restriction, the expected time to reach $m$ blocks is at most $\sum_{j>m}2/[j(j-1)]=2/m$. <Markov inequality>, followed by increasing the restriction, gives $\mathbb P(B_t>m)\leq2/(mt)$. Let $m\to\infty$, then use rational times and monotonicity to obtain the assertion simultaneously for every $t>0$.

= Entry count of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$N_s(t)$}

= Entry count
{synonym}

= Markov-chain entry count
{synonym}

The entry count $N_s(t)$ counts jumps of a <continuous-time Markov chain> into state $s$ by time $t$, excluding initial occupation. It is a <counting process>. For homogeneous <transition intensities>, its mean has derivative $\sum_{i\ne s}p_{ri}(t)q_{is}$ conditional on initial state $r$, so its <expected value> is the sum of incoming rates times the expected <occupation times of a continuous-time Markov chain>. This density is an expected count rate, not necessarily a normalized <probability density function>.

= Occupation time of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\int_0^t\mathbf1\{Z(u)=s\}\,du$}

= Occupation times of a continuous-time Markov chain
{synonym}

= Occupation time
{synonym}

The occupation time records the duration spent in a state of a <continuous-time Markov chain>. Conditional on $Z(0)=r$, its <expected value> is $\int_0^t p_{rs}(u)\,du$, where $p_{rs}$ is the <transition probability>.

= Occupation time at an interior state of a variable-rate symmetric walk
{parent=Occupation time of a continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\mathbb E_iI=2i(N-i)/(N\lambda_i)$}

For a <continuous-time Markov chain> with absorbing endpoints $0,N$ and rates $q_{j,j\pm1}=\lambda_j/2$, the jump chain is a <simple symmetric random walk>. On leaving $i$, absorption before returning has probability $p_i=\tfrac12(1/i+1/(N-i))$, by <gambler's ruin> on each side. The <geometric return count before absorption> and independent rate-$\lambda_i$ <holding times> give $\mathbb E_iI=1/(p_i\lambda_i)$. The rates at other states affect elapsed excursion times but not their return probabilities.

= Expected finite-horizon occupation time
{parent=Occupation time of a continuous-time Markov chain}
{title2=$\mathbb E_iO_j(T)=\int_0^T(e^{tQ})_{ij}\,dt$}

For a finite-state homogeneous <continuous-time Markov chain> with <generator matrix> $Q$, integrate the indicator of occupying state $j$ over $[0,T]$. Interchanging the bounded time integral and <expected value> gives the displayed integral of <transition probabilities>. It counts all visits, not only the first <holding time>. Summing over states gives $T$. A zero <eigenvalue> of $Q$ does not cause a problem for this integral, although it prevents casually writing it with an <inverse matrix> $Q^{-1}$.

= Holding time
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

The holding time of a <continuous-time Markov chain> is the time spent in its current state before the next jump. In state $i$ it has an <exponential distribution> of rate equal to the total outgoing rate $q_i$.

= Mean holding time from a transition intensity matrix
{parent=Holding time}
{title2=$E(T_r)=-1/q_{rr}$}

A state's <holding time> in a time-homogeneous <continuous-time Markov chain> is exponentially distributed with rate $-q_{rr}>0$. Its mean is $-1/q_{rr}$; the <probability> that its next jump is to state $s$ is $q_{rs}/(-q_{rr})$. An absorbing state has infinite holding time.

= Confidence interval for an inverse rate
{parent=Mean holding time from a transition intensity matrix}
{title2=$[\lambda_L,\lambda_U]\mapsto[1/\lambda_U,1/\lambda_L]$}

For an <exponential distribution> with positive rate $\lambda$, the mean is $1/\lambda$. A <confidence interval> for the rate transforms to one for the mean by reversing and inverting its endpoints. The monotonic transformation preserves the coverage event; it does not give a <prediction interval> for one realized <holding time>.

= Master equation
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki}

For transition rate $w_{ij}$ from state $i$ to state $j$, the master equation is
$$
\dot P_i=\sum_j(w_{ji}P_j-w_{ij}P_i).
$$

= Entropy production rate of a Markov chain
{parent=Master equation}

For edge current $J_{ij}=w_{ij}P_i-w_{ji}P_j$, the total entropy production rate is
$$
\dot S_{\rm tot}=\frac12\sum_{i,j}J_{ij}
\log\frac{w_{ij}P_i}{w_{ji}P_j}\geq0.
$$
It vanishes precisely when every occupied edge satisfies detailed balance.

= Continuous-time random walk
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki}

A continuous-time random walk waits a random time at each site before jumping. Constant outgoing rates produce an exponential residence time, while the relative transition rates determine the destination of the next jump.

= Coalescing random walks
{parent=Continuous-time random walk}

Particles perform independent <continuous-time random walks> until they meet, after which they share one path. A finite initial particle set remains finite and its cardinality cannot increase. They arise as the common ancestors of sites in a <voter model>. On the <square lattice> every pair eventually coalesces, because the difference walk is <recurrent Markov chain>.

= Martingale problem for a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

A finite-state process has generator $Q$ exactly when, for every function $f$ on the state space,
$$
f(X_t)-f(X_0)-\int_0^t(Qf)(X_s)\,ds
$$
is a martingale with respect to the process filtration.

= Transition-rate matrix
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Q-matrix
{c}
{synonym}

A Q-matrix has nonnegative off-diagonal entries and row sums zero. Its diagonal entry is the negative total rate out of the state.

= Transition semigroup of a continuous-time Markov chain
{title2=$P(t)$}
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

= Transition probability matrix
{synonym}

For a <continuous-time Markov chain>, $P_{ij}(t)=\mathbb P_i(X_t=j)$ and the matrices satisfy $P(s+t)=P(s)P(t)$. On a finite state space with <Q-matrix> $Q$, $P(t)=e^{tQ}$.

= Poisson-sampled continuous-time Markov chain
{title2=$P_\lambda=\lambda(\lambda I-Q)^{-1}$}
{parent=Transition semigroup of a continuous-time Markov chain}

Sample a finite-state <continuous-time Markov chain> with generator $Q$ at the independent arrival times of a rate-$\lambda$ <Poisson process>. The sampled states form a discrete-time Markov chain with transition matrix
$$
P_\lambda
=\int_0^\infty\lambda e^{-\lambda t}e^{tQ}\,dt
=\lambda(\lambda I-Q)^{-1}.
$$
Its invariant distributions are exactly those of the continuous-time chain.

= Kolmogorov equations
{c}
{parent=Transition semigroup of a continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki}

For a finite-state <continuous-time Markov chain> with <Q-matrix> $Q$, the backward and forward equations are $P'(t)=QP(t)$ and $P'(t)=P(t)Q$, respectively.

= Markov reward model
{c}
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki}

A Markov reward model attaches a reward rate to each state and integrates it over the time spent there.

= Markov jump-process generator
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

For transition rates $q_{nm}$, the generator acts on a function $f$ by
$$
(Lf)(n)=\sum_{m\ne n}q_{nm}\bigl(f(m)-f(n)\bigr).
$$
Whenever the expectations are finite,
$$
\frac d{dt}\mathbb E[f(N_t)]=\mathbb E[(Lf)(N_t)].
$$
Choosing polynomial functions $f$ gives differential equations for the moments.

= Exponential martingale from a jump generator
{parent=Markov jump-process generator}
{title2=$Z_t=g(X_t)\exp\left(-\int_0^t\frac{Lg(X_{s-})}{g(X_{s-})}\,ds\right)$}

For a positive function $g$ in the domain of a <Markov jump-process generator>, the generator martingale for $g(X_t)$ and <semimartingale integration by parts> give the displayed <local martingale>. Suitable finite-horizon deterministic bounds on $g$ and $Lg/g$ make it a true <martingale>. For a <single marked exponential jump process>, take $g(x)=e^{\theta x}$. Then the exponent's compensating process is $r_\theta(t\wedge T)$, where $r_\theta=(e^\theta-1)/\theta-1$ for $\theta\ne0$ and $r_0=0$; it is continuous adapted and hence <predictable>.

= Forward operator of a Markov jump process
{title2=$L^*$}
{parent=Markov jump-process generator}

For jumps $x\mapsto x+\nu_r$ of rates $a_r(x)$, the forward operator acts on a probability mass function by
$$
(L^*p)(x)=\sum_r\left[a_r(x-\nu_r)p(x-\nu_r)-a_r(x)p(x)\right].
$$
It is adjoint to the <Markov jump-process generator> $Lf(x)=\sum_ra_r(x)[f(x+\nu_r)-f(x)]$.

= Holding-time reversal along a fixed CTMC jump path
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

Conditioned on a finite jump path $i_0,\ldots,i_n$, the holding times are independent exponentials with the corresponding rates. Multiplying the probability of occupying $i_n$ at time $t$ by $q_{i_n}$ turns its simplex density into a product symmetric under reversal of the time portions and the state sequence.

= Recurrence equivalence for a CTMC and its fixed-time skeleton
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

For $h>0$, the fixed-time skeleton has matrix $P(h)$. If $q_i$ is the holding rate, then for $nh\leq t\leq(n+1)h$,
$$
e^{-q_ih}p_{ii}(nh)\leq p_{ii}(t)
\leq e^{q_ih}p_{ii}((n+1)h).
$$
Thus $\int_0^\infty p_{ii}(t)dt$ diverges exactly when $\sum_np_{ii}(nh)$ does, proving recurrence equivalence for an irreducible chain.

= Jump chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

The jump chain records the successive states visited by a continuous-time Markov chain while discarding its holding times.

= Reflected nearest-neighbour random walk
{parent=Jump chain}

On $\mathbb N$, let the chain jump from every $i\geq1$ to $i+1$ with probability $p$ and to $i-1$ with probability $1-p$, and jump from zero to one. It is recurrent exactly when $p\leq1/2$; at $p=1/2$ it is null recurrent.

= Invariant-measure transfer between a jump chain and a CTMC
{parent=Jump chain}

For holding rates $q_i$, a measure $\pi$ satisfies $\pi Q=0$ exactly when $\mu_i=q_i\pi_i$ is invariant for the jump chain. Thus $\pi_i=\mu_i/q_i$ when normalization is possible.

= Slow holding rates destroy positive recurrence
{parent=Invariant-measure transfer between a jump chain and a CTMC}

Even when the jump chain has invariant probability $\mu$, the continuous-time invariant measure $\mu_i/q_i$ may have infinite mass if holding rates become too small, producing infinite mean return time.

= Fast holding rates create continuous-time positive recurrence
{parent=Invariant-measure transfer between a jump chain and a CTMC}

A recurrent jump chain with infinite invariant measure $\mu$ can yield a positive-recurrent continuous-time chain when $\sum_i\mu_i/q_i<\infty$.

= Nonexplosion from recurrent visits to a slow state
{parent=Jump chain}

If the jump chain visits a state of finite holding rate infinitely often, the sum of its independent positive holding times at that state diverges almost surely, preventing explosion.

= Irreducibility and recurrence pass between a CTMC and its jump chain
{parent=Jump chain}

For a <continuous-time Markov chain> whose jump times tend to infinity, the chain and its <jump chain> have the same directed paths of positive-probability jumps. They are therefore <irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible] simultaneously, and a state is <recurrent state>[recurrent] for one exactly when it is recurrent for the other: the event of returning after departure depends only on the sequence of visited states.

= Explosion of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

Explosion occurs when infinitely many jumps take place in finite time.

Explosion is determined by whether the successive holding times of the <continuous-time Markov chain> have a finite sum.

= Nonexplosion under uniformly bounded jump rates
{parent=Explosion of a continuous-time Markov chain}

If every holding rate of a <continuous-time Markov chain> is at most $C<\infty$, its holding times can be coupled as $E_n/q_{Y_n}\geq E_n/C$, where the $E_n$ are independent rate-one <exponential distribution>[exponential random variables]. The <strong law of large numbers> gives $\sum_nE_n=\infty$ almost surely, so the jump times tend to infinity and the chain cannot <explosion of a continuous-time Markov chain>[explode].

= Explosion by summable holding times
{parent=Explosion of a continuous-time Markov chain}

Conditional on a jump path $(Y_n)$, if $\sum_n1/q_{Y_n}<\infty$, then the sum of exponential holding times has finite expectation and is finite almost surely.

= Explosion of an upward-biased walk with geometrically increasing rates
{parent=Explosion of a continuous-time Markov chain}

Suppose a transient nearest-neighbour jump chain on $\mathbb N$ has uniformly bounded expected visits to each state and the holding rate at $i$ grows geometrically. Then
$$
\mathbb E\sum_n\frac1{q_{Y_n}}
=\sum_i\frac{\mathbb E V_i}{q_i}<\infty,
$$
so the continuous-time chain explodes almost surely.

= Invariant distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

For a finite-state chain with transition semigroup $P(t)=e^{tQ}$, a probability row vector $\pi$ is invariant when $\pi P(t)=\pi$ for every $t\geq0$. Equivalently,
$$
\pi Q=0.
$$

= Nonuniqueness of invariant distributions of a finite Markov chain
{parent=Invariant distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain}

Every closed communicating class of a finite Markov chain has its own invariant probability distribution. All invariant distributions are convex combinations of those class distributions. In particular, a finite irreducible chain has a unique invariant distribution, while a reducible chain may have many.

= Invariant distribution of an explosive chain
{parent=Invariant distribution of a continuous-time Markov chain}

An explosive chain can have a summable solution of $\pi Q=0$ despite being transient. Nonexplosion is needed for the usual equivalence between invariant distributions and positive recurrence.

= Batch-birth linear-death process
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}

A batch-birth linear-death process has transitions
$$
n\longrightarrow n+k\quad\hbox{at rate }\lambda,
\qquad
n\longrightarrow n-1\quad\hbox{at rate }\beta n.
$$
Writing $P_n(t)=\mathbb P(N_t=n)$ and taking $P_j=0$ for $j<0$, its master equation is
$$
\dot P_n=\lambda P_{n-k}+\beta(n+1)P_{n+1}-(\lambda+\beta n)P_n.
$$

= Moments of a batch-birth linear-death process
{parent=Batch-birth linear-death process}

For $m=\mathbb E N$ and $v=\operatorname{var}N$, the Markov jump-process generator applied to $n$ and $n^2$ gives
$$
m'=k\lambda-\beta m,
\qquad
v'=k^2\lambda+\beta m-2\beta v.
$$
Consequently
$$
m\longrightarrow\frac{k\lambda}{\beta},
\qquad
v\longrightarrow\frac{k(k+1)\lambda}{2\beta}.
$$

= Birth-death process
{parent=Continuous-time Markov chain}
{wiki=Birth–death_process}

A birth-death process moves only between neighbouring nonnegative integer states, with state-dependent birth and death rates.

= Birth process
{parent=Birth-death process}
{wiki}

= Pure birth process
{synonym}

A pure birth process on $\mathbb N_0$ jumps only from $n$ to $n+1$, at a strictly positive rate $\lambda_n$. Its successive holding times are independent exponential variables of rates $\lambda_n$.

= Exponential martingale of a pure birth process
{parent=Birth process}
{title2=$M_t=\exp\!\left(\theta X_t-(e^\theta-1)\int_0^t\lambda(X_s)ds\right)$}

For a <pure birth process> with fixed initial state and finite statewise rates, the jump-count compensator gives $dM=(e^\theta-1)M_{-}[dN-\lambda(X_{-})dt]$, so $M$ is a <local martingale> up to explosion. If $\lambda\le C$, a rate-$C$ Poisson candidate clock with accepted jumps of probability $\lambda(X_{-})/C$ prevents explosion and dominates the jump count. Its finite exponential moments dominate every stopped $M$ on each finite horizon, upgrading the local martingale to a true martingale. A random initial state requires $\mathbb E e^{\theta X_0}<\infty$; uniformly bounded rates do not supply this missing initial integrability.

= Logistic cell-division process
{parent=Birth process}
{title2=$q_N(k,k+1)=k(1-k/N)$}

For a carrying capacity $N$, each of $k$ cells splits into two at rate $1-k/N$, so the population increases by one at the displayed total rate and is absorbed at $N$. Starting from population density $p\in(0,1)$, its normalized density converges on finite horizons to $p e^t/(1-p+p e^t)$ by the <Kurtz fluid limit theorem>.

// Target: analysis.bigb

= Nonexplosion criterion for a pure birth process
{parent=Birth process}

A pure birth process is nonexplosive exactly when
$$
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac1{\lambda_n}=\infty.
$$
Its explosion time is the sum of independent exponential holding times. If the displayed series converges, that sum has finite expectation. If it diverges, the Laplace transform of the sum is
$$
\prod_n\frac{\lambda_n}{\lambda_n+s}=0,
$$
forcing the sum to be infinite almost surely.

= Linear birth process with immigration
{parent=Birth process}

The pure birth process with rates $\lambda_n=\alpha n+\beta$, where $\alpha,\beta>0$, combines linear reproduction with immigration. Starting from zero, its mean satisfies
$$
m'(t)=\alpha m(t)+\beta,
\qquad
m(0)=0,
$$
and hence
$$
\mathbb EN(t)=\frac{\beta}{\alpha}(e^{\alpha t}-1).
$$

= Linear birth process with catastrophes
{parent=Linear birth process with immigration}
{title2=$q_{n,n+1}=\lambda(n+1),\quad q_{n,0}=\mu\ (n\ge1)$}

Add independent rate-$\mu$ resets to zero to a <linear birth process with immigration>. The resulting irreducible <continuous-time Markov chain> is nonexplosive by domination of births and finiteness of Poisson reset counts. When $\mu=\lambda$, an excursion from one reaches $n$ with probability $2/(n+1)$, so its <jump chain> is recurrent with infinite expected jump count. The elapsed return-cycle time instead has mean
$$
\lambda^{-1}+\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{2}{\lambda(n+1)(n+2)}=2/\lambda.
$$
Thus the continuous-time chain is positive recurrent while its jump chain is null recurrent. Its stationary probabilities are $\pi_0=1/2$ and $\pi_n=1/[(n+1)(n+2)]$ for $n\ge1$.

= Yule process
{c}
{parent=Birth-death process}
{wiki}

The Yule process is the pure birth process with rate $n\lambda$ in population state $n$. Starting from one individual, it is nonexplosive because the independent holding times have divergent total almost surely.

= Mean population of a Yule process
{title2=$\mathbb E X_t=e^{\lambda t}$}
{parent=Yule process}

For the Yule process starting from one individual, the generator applied to $f(n)=n$ gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\mathbb E X_t=\lambda\mathbb E X_t,
\qquad \mathbb E X_0=1,
$$
and hence $\mathbb E X_t=e^{\lambda t}$.

= Conditional Yule birth times
{parent=Yule process}

Given $X_t=n+1$, the $n$ birth times in a Yule process have the law of the order statistics of independent variables with density
$$
f(x)=\frac{\lambda e^{\lambda x}}{e^{\lambda t}-1},
\qquad0\leq x\leq t.
$$
This follows by dividing the joint holding-time density by
$\mathbb P(X_t=n+1)=e^{-\lambda t}(1-e^{-\lambda t})^n$.

= Extinction probability of a linear birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death process}

For individual birth and death rates $\lambda,\mu$ and one initial individual, the finite-time extinction probability satisfies
$$
h'=(h-1)(\lambda h-\mu),\qquad h(0)=0.
$$
When $\lambda\ne\mu$,
$$
h(t)=\frac{\mu(1-e^{-(\lambda-\mu)t})}
{\lambda-\mu e^{-(\lambda-\mu)t}}.
$$

= Detailed balance for a birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death process}

For birth rates $b_i$ and death rates $d_i$, a reversible measure satisfies
$$
\pi_i b_i=\pi_{i+1}d_{i+1}.
$$
Thus $\pi_{i+1}/\pi_i=b_i/d_{i+1}$, and it is an invariant distribution when the resulting measure is summable and the chain is nonexplosive.

= Birth-death master equation
{parent=Birth-death process}

For birth rates $b_n$, death rates $d_n$, and state probabilities $P_n$,
$$
\dot P_n=b_{n-1}P_{n-1}+d_{n+1}P_{n+1}-(b_n+d_n)P_n.
$$

= First-moment equation of a birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death master equation}

Since births and deaths change the state by $+1$ and $-1$,
$$\frac d{dt}\mathbb{E} N_t=\mathbb{E}[b_{N_t}-d_{N_t}].$$

= Moment equations of a linear birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death master equation}

For per-capita birth and death rates $B$ and $D$, the rates are $b_n=Bn$ and $d_n=Dn$. If $\mu=\mathbb EN_t$ and $\sigma^2=\operatorname{var}N_t$, then
$$
\mu'=(B-D)\mu,
\qquad
(\sigma^2)'=2(B-D)\sigma^2+(B+D)\mu.
$$
The <expected value> sees only the net growth rate $B-D$, whereas the <variance> also sees the total event rate $B+D$.

= Factorial moment
{parent=Birth-death master equation}
{wiki}

The $r$th falling factorial moment is $\mathbb{E}[(N)_r]$, where $(N)_r=N(N-1)\cdots(N-r+1)$.

= Factorial-moment inversion
{parent=Factorial moment}
{title2=$\Pr(X=k)=\frac1{k!}\sum_{r\ge0}\frac{(-1)^r\mathbb E(X)_{k+r}}{r!}$}

For a nonnegative integer-valued <random variable>, the displayed inversion is valid when $\mathbb E(X)_s s^k/s!\to0$. Indeed the gap between consecutive <Jordan-Bonferroni exact-occurrence inequalities> is $\mathbb E(X)_{k+m+1}/[k!(m+1)!]$, which tends to zero under this hypothesis. No exchange of an infinite signed sum with an <expectation> is needed.

= Poisson factorial moments
{parent=Factorial moment}
{c}

If $N$ is Poisson with mean $\lambda$, then $\mathbb{E}[(N)_r]=\lambda^r$; in particular, $\mathbb{E}N=\lambda$ and $\mathbb{E}[N(N-1)]=\lambda^2$.

= Factorial-moment criterion for Poisson convergence
{parent=Poisson factorial moments}
{title2=$\mathbb E(X_n)_k\to\lambda^k$}

If nonnegative integer-valued <random variables> $X_n$ have all fixed <factorial moments> converging to $\lambda^k$, then their <convergence in distribution> is to the <Poisson distribution> with mean $\lambda$. The first-moment bound gives <uniform tightness>, and bounds on higher moments give <uniform integrability> of each fixed lower moment. Every subsequential limit therefore has the indicated <factorial moments>. For such a limit $X$ and $t\geq0$, the nonnegative binomial expansion and the <monotone convergence theorem> give $\mathbb E(1+t)^X=\sum_{k\geq0}t^k\lambda^k/k!=e^{\lambda t}$. This identifies its <moment generating function> as that of the <Poisson distribution>, uniquely determining the limit.

= Poisson moment closure for a birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death process}
{c}

Approximating the population law by a Poisson law replaces its first two factorial moments by $\lambda$ and $\lambda^2$, closing a quadratic first-moment equation as a Riccati equation for $\lambda$.

= Diffusion approximation of a birth-death process
{parent=Birth-death process}

For unit jumps with birth and death rates $b(x),d(x)$, the Fokker--Planck drift and diffusion in the convention
$$\partial_tP=-\partial_x(uP)+\partial_x^2(DP)$$
are $u=b-d$ and $D=(b+d)/2$.

= Linear noise approximation
{parent=Diffusion approximation of a birth-death process}
{wiki}

Near a stable deterministic equilibrium, replace the drift by its linearization and the diffusion coefficient by its equilibrium value. The resulting Ornstein-Uhlenbeck approximation has a stationary <normal distribution>.

= Chemical diffusion matrix
{parent=Linear noise approximation}
{title2=$B=\sum_ra_r\nu_r\nu_r^{\mathsf T},\quad D_{ij}=B_{ij}/(\mu_i\mu_j)$}

A reaction's <stoichiometric vector> outer product multiplied by its <reaction propensity function> is its contribution to the infinitesimal state-increment covariance. Summing over channels gives $B$. Its normalized form $D$ divides by the two mean copy numbers. The <Fokker-Planck equation> writes the associated second-derivative term with $B/2$; the covariance evolution and normalized stationary Lyapunov equation instead use $B$ and $D$ themselves. A simultaneous unit loss of two species contributes positive cross noise even when their long-time stationary covariance is negative.

= Normalized stationary fluctuation-dissipation relation for a reaction network
{parent=Linear noise approximation}
{title2=$M\eta+\eta M^{\mathsf T}=D$}

Let $A$ be the drift Jacobian at a stable mean-field equilibrium, $S=\operatorname{diag}(\mu_i)$, and $B=\sum_ra_r\nu_r\nu_r^{\mathsf T}$ the reaction noise matrix. For normalized fluctuations, put $M=-S^{-1}AS$, $D=S^{-1}BS^{-1}$, and $\eta_{ij}=\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)/(\mu_i\mu_j)$. Stationarity of the linear-noise covariance gives the displayed <continuous Lyapunov equation>. It applies to driven reaction networks without requiring thermal equilibrium. A mode with zero restoring eigenvalue and nonzero noise has no stationary covariance.

// Target: mathematical-biology.bigb

= Feedback attenuation of gene-expression noise
{parent=Normalized stationary fluctuation-dissipation relation for a reaction network}
{title2=$\eta_{22}\simeq\frac1{Hm_2}+\frac{\beta_2}{H(\beta_1+\beta_2H)m_1}$}

For RNA birth/death rates $\lambda_1,\beta_1x_1$ and protein rates $\lambda_2x_1f(x_2),\beta_2x_2$, linearize at stationary means $m_1,m_2$. Put $H=1-\partial\log f/\partial\log x_2=1+h$. The relative-fluctuation drift is $\begin{pmatrix}-\beta_1&0\\\beta_2&-\beta_2H\end{pmatrix}$ and the diffusion matrix is $\operatorname{diag}(2\beta_1/m_1,2\beta_2/m_2)$. The stationary covariance equation gives the displayed normalized protein variance. Its extrinsic part factors as $H^{-2}[\beta_2H/(\beta_1+\beta_2H)]m_1^{-1}$: static susceptibility squared times a time-averaging factor times environmental noise. Faster response alone weakens averaging; feedback reduces susceptibility and thereby lowers noise. The formula is exact for affine rates when $H=1$, and is a <linear noise approximation> for nonlinear feedback.

= Ornstein-Uhlenbeck fluctuation limit at a stable population equilibrium
{c}
{parent=Linear noise approximation}
{title2=$dX_t=-\alpha X_tdt+\alpha\sqrt{2/\beta}\,dB_t$}

For a unit-jump population chain with one-step probabilities $\alpha k/n$ and $\beta k^2/n^2$, its equilibrium density is $u_0=\alpha/\beta$. Speed time by $n$ and center and scale the population as $(k-nu_0)/\sqrt n$. The accelerated conditional drift is $-\alpha x-\beta x^2/\sqrt n$, and the second moment is $2\alpha^2/\beta+3\alpha x/\sqrt n+\beta x^2/n$. Their compact-set limits give the displayed <Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process>. If $2\alpha^2<\beta$, the invalid-probability threshold stays a fixed positive density distance above equilibrium. Before that threshold, the generator of $x^2$ is at most one because its drift contribution is nonpositive. The stopped <Lyapunov function> bound proves <compact containment> and makes the probability of hitting that threshold on a fixed accelerated horizon tend to zero. This supplies the global hypotheses of the <diffusion approximation theorem>.

= Mean drift from a Fokker--Planck equation
{parent=Diffusion approximation of a birth-death process}

If boundary fluxes decay sufficiently fast, two integrations by parts give
$$\frac d{dt}\mathbb{E} X_t=\mathbb{E}[u(X_t)],$$
while the diffusion term contributes zero to the first moment.

= Birth-death recurrence classification
{parent=Birth-death process}

For constant birth rate $\lambda$ and death rate $\delta$ away from zero, the chain is transient when $\lambda>\delta$, null recurrent when $\lambda=\delta$, and positive recurrent when $\lambda<\delta$.

= M-M-1 queue
{parent=Birth-death process}
{c}
{wiki=M/M/1_queue}

The queue length in an $M/M/1$ queue is a birth-death process with constant arrival rate $\lambda$ and service rate $\mu$ while nonempty.

= Stationary workload of an exponential-payload queue
{parent=M-M-1 queue}
{title2=$\mathbb P(W\geq r)=\rho e^{-(1-\rho)r},\quad r>0$}

With packet arrival rate lambda, independent exponential payloads of mean one and byte-service rate C, let $\rho=\lambda/C<1$. The number N of unfinished packets has law $(1-\rho)\rho^n$. Conditional on N=n, the remaining payloads are n independent unit-mean exponentials, including the in-service residual by memorylessness. Summing their <Laplace transforms> gives $\mathbb E e^{-sW}=(1-\rho)(1+s)/(1+s-\rho)$. Thus W has mass $1-\rho$ at zero and, conditional on positivity, is exponential with rate $1-\rho$. The probability of at least r headers is instead $\rho^r$ for integer r.

= M-M-1 queue with state-dependent admission
{parent=M-M-1 queue}
{c}

If potential arrivals form a Poisson process of rate $\lambda$ and an arrival seeing $n$ customers joins with probability $p(n)$, the queue length is a <birth-death process> with birth rate $b_n=\lambda p(n)$ and death rate $d_n=\mu$ for $n\geq1$. Its stationary ratios, when normalizable, are
$$
\frac{\pi_{n+1}}{\pi_n}=\frac{\lambda p(n)}\mu.
$$

= Positive recurrence of an M-M-1 queue
{parent=M-M-1 queue}

An $M/M/1$ queue is positive recurrent exactly when its traffic intensity $\rho=\lambda/\mu$ is below one.

= Stationary distribution of an M-M-1 queue
{parent=M-M-1 queue}

When $\rho<1$, the equilibrium queue length has distribution $\pi_n=(1-\rho)\rho^n$ and mean $\rho/(1-\rho)$.

= Burke's theorem
{parent=M-M-1 queue}
{c}
{wiki}

= Burke theorem
{c}
{synonym}

At equilibrium, the departure process of a stable $M/M/1$ queue is Poisson with the same rate as the arrival process.

= M-M-1 queue with Bernoulli feedback
{parent=M-M-1 queue}
{c}

If a served customer returns with probability $p$, thinning service completions gives effective external departure rate $\mu(1-p)$ for the queue-length process.

= Poisson output theorem for a feedback queue
{parent=M-M-1 queue with Bernoulli feedback}
{c}

In equilibrium, a Bernoulli-feedback $M/M/1$ queue has the same queue-length process as an ordinary queue with service rate $\mu(1-p)$, so its external departures form a Poisson process of rate $\lambda$.

= Mean busy period of an M-M-1 queue
{parent=M-M-1 queue}

Starting with one customer, the mean busy period of an $M/M/1$ queue is
$$
\mathbb EB=\frac1{\mu-\lambda}
$$
when $\lambda<\mu$. It is infinite when $\lambda\geq\mu$.

= M-M-infinity queue
{parent=Birth-death process}
{c}
{wiki=M/M/∞_queue}

= M-M-∞ queue
{c}
{synonym}

An $M/M/\infty$ queue has <Poisson process>[Poisson] arrivals of rate $\lambda$, <independent random variables>[independent] exponential service times of rate $\mu$, and infinitely many servers. Its occupancy is a <birth-death process> with birth rate $\lambda$ and death rate $n\mu$ in state $n$.

= Stationary distribution of an M-M-infinity queue
{parent=M-M-infinity queue}

For every $\lambda,\mu>0$, an $M/M/\infty$ queue is positive recurrent and has the <Poisson distribution> with mean $\rho=\lambda/\mu$ as its unique stationary distribution:
$$
\pi_n=e^{-\rho}\frac{\rho^n}{n!}.
$$

= Burke theorem for an M-M-infinity queue
{parent=M-M-infinity queue}
{c}

In stationarity, the departure process of an $M/M/\infty$ queue is a <Poisson process> of rate $\lambda$. By time reversibility, the departures before any fixed time are independent of the occupancy at that time.

= Transient distribution of an M-M-infinity queue
{parent=M-M-infinity queue}

If $X_0=n$ and $p_t=e^{-\mu t}$, then
$$
X_t\overset{d}{=}
\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p_t)
+\operatorname{Poisson}\!\left(\frac\lambda\mu(1-p_t)\right),
$$
with independent summands. The binomial term counts surviving initial customers; <Poisson thinning> gives the surviving later arrivals.

= Mean busy period of an M-M-infinity queue
{parent=M-M-infinity queue}

Writing $\rho=\lambda/\mu$, the stationary empty probability is $e^{-\rho}$. Alternating idle periods of mean $1/\lambda$ with busy periods and applying the renewal-reward theorem gives
$$
\mathbb EB=\frac{e^\rho-1}{\lambda}.
$$

= M-M-s-K queue
{title2=$M/M/s/K$}
{c}
{parent=Birth-death process}

An $M/M/s/K$ queue has arrivals forming a <Poisson process> of rate $\lambda$, $s$ servers with independent service times having an <exponential distribution> of rate $\mu$, and total system capacity $K\geq s$. Its occupancy is the finite <birth-death process> on $\{0,\ldots,K\}$ with
$$
b_n=\lambda\mathbf1_{\{n<K\}},
\qquad
d_n=\mu\min(n,s).
$$

= Stationary distribution of an M-M-s-K queue
{parent=M-M-s-K queue}

Put $\rho=\lambda/\mu$. The unique stationary distribution of an $M/M/s/K$ queue is
$$
\pi_n=\frac1Z
\begin{cases}
\rho^n/n!,&0\leq n\leq s,\\
\rho^n/(s!s^{n-s}),&s<n\leq K,
\end{cases}
$$
where $Z$ is the sum of the displayed weights. Adjacent terms satisfy <detailed balance for a birth-death process>, so the queue is reversible in equilibrium.

= Finite Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Markov chain ergodic theorem
{c}
{parent=Finite Markov chain}

For an irreducible finite <Markov chain> with <stationary distribution> $\pi$, the fraction of visits to any state $j$ converges almost surely to $\pi_j$, for every initial state. More generally, the empirical average of a function of the state converges to its average under $\pi$. Aperiodicity is needed for convergence of the one-time distributions, but not for this empirical-frequency conclusion. Regeneration at returns to a state proves the conclusion by applying the strong law to independent excursion lengths and excursion rewards.

= Constant nonzero drift is impossible on a finite Markov chain
{parent=Finite Markov chain}
{title2=$Pf-f\not\equiv-\delta\quad(\delta>0)$}

For a real function $f$ on a finite state space, a state minimizing $f$ satisfies $Pf\geq f$, so strictly negative drift cannot hold at every state. Equivalently, telescoping the <expected value> would give $\mathbb E_x f(X_n)=f(x)-n\delta$, contradicting boundedness of $f$. Constant negative drift is useful before a target is reached; it must be restricted to the states outside that target.

= Communicating class
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Open communicating class
{parent=Communicating class}

A <communicating class> of a <Markov chain> is open when a state in the class has positive probability of transitioning to a state outside it. Once a path leaves, it cannot return to the class: a return path would make the outside state communicate with the class. Every open class consists of <transient states>. Conversely, a finite transient <communicating class> is open; an infinite <closed communicating class> can be transient.

= Communicating states
{parent=Communicating class}

= Communicating state
{synonym}

Two states of a <Markov chain> communicate when each can be reached from the other with positive probability in finitely many steps.

= Closed communicating class
{parent=Communicating class}

A communicating class is closed when no transition from it can reach a state outside it.

= Transient communicating class
{parent=Communicating class}

In a finite chain, a communicating class that is not closed is transient: after it is left, it need not be visited again and is eventually left almost surely.

= Period of a state in a Markov chain
{title2=$d(i)$}
{parent=Communicating class}

The period of a state $i$ is the greatest common divisor of its positive-probability return times:
$$
d(i)=\gcd\{n\geq1:P^n(i,i)>0\}.
$$
A state is aperiodic when its period is one.

= Return-free state in a Markov chain
{parent=Period of a state in a Markov chain}

A state can have no positive-probability return at any positive time, for example when its transition-matrix column is zero. Its return-time set is empty. The convention $\gcd\varnothing=0$ assigns period zero; conventions defining period only for a nonempty return set instead leave it undefined. Such a state must not be called aperiodic merely because its communicating class is a singleton.

= Period is constant on a communicating class
{parent=Period of a state in a Markov chain}

If communicating states $i,j$ can be joined in $r$ and $s$ steps, then $r+s$ is a return time to $i$, and adjoining any return loop of length $n$ at $j$ makes $r+n+s$ another. Their difference shows $d(i)\mid n$, hence $d(i)\mid d(j)$; symmetry gives equality.

= Stationary distribution
{parent=Markov chain}
{wiki}

= Invariant distribution
{synonym}

A stationary distribution $\pi$ satisfies $\pi P=\pi$.

= Time-reversed Markov chain
{parent=Markov chain}

For a stationary <Markov chain> with transition matrix $P$ and stationary distribution $\pi$, the reversed transition matrix is
$$
P^*(x,y)=\frac{\pi(y)P(y,x)}{\pi(x)}.
$$
It is stochastic and has the same stationary distribution.
